 has spent twenty years arguing about how much credit to give a shot. The argument runs in one direction ‚Äî toward weighting more, counting less."}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["It started with possession proxies that valued every attempt the same. ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Corsi"}),' (Tim Barnes, writing as "Vic Ferrari," circa 2007) summed every shot attempt at the net ‚Äî goals, shots-on-goal, missed shots, and blocked shots ‚Äî as a proxy for territorial play. ',(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Fenwick"})," (Matt Fenwick, 2007) dropped the blocked shots, on the argument that a blocked attempt isn't the same kind of event as one that actually reaches the net."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The next move was to weight the surviving attempts by quality.",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:" Dangerous Fenwick"}),' (Dawson Sprigings, writing as "DTMAboutHeart" on Hockey-Graphs) used shot location and type to weight each unblocked attempt by its conversion likelihood ‚Äî an early public attempt at what would become ',(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"expected goals (xG)"}),". War-on-ice's scoring chances and Eric Tulsky's shot-quality work pushed in the same direction. By the mid-2010s, MoneyPuck, Evolving-Hockey, Hockeyviz, and Natural Stat Trick were each shipping their own xG variants ‚Äî different models, same idea: replace shot count with shot probability."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Running alongside that, a different idea. In 2014"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"https://tangotiger.com/index.php/site/comments/introducing-weighted-shots-differential-aka-tango",className:"text-accent hover:underline",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",children:"Tom Tango proposed weighted shots"})," ","‚Äî not a quality model at all, but a blunt reweighting of outcomes: goals, plus one-fifth of a credit for every other attempt. His argument was purely predictive. Regressed across seasons, non-goal attempts foreshadow future goals at roughly a fifth the rate that goals themselves do, so that is what they should be worth."," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"https://puckplusplus.com/2014/12/10/score-adjusted-weighted-shots/",className:"text-accent hover:underline",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",children:"Puck++ later added score and venue adjustments"})," ","and found the result beat score-adjusted Corsi out of sample. No shot location, no shot quality, no player adjustment ‚Äî and it worked."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["That left a residual question: what about the goals themselves? An xG model gives a 0.30-xG shot the same credit whether it goes in or not ‚Äî that's the whole point of ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"expected"}),". But once you're building a single shot-driving stat for player evaluation, it's tempting to add goals back as their own term, on the theory that finishing is real and persistent."," ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"wxG"}),", the metric this page is about, takes that theory seriously: a small attempt credit, plus xG, plus a goal premium, built on a talent-adjusted shot-quality model rather than Tango's flat weighting."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"wxG and Tango's weighted shots are different metrics with different arguments behind them, and the naming is worth separating clearly: his gives every non-goal attempt a flat fifth of a goal and knows nothing about where the puck came from; wxG puts the bulk of its weight on a talent-adjusted xG model and gives non-goal attempts about a thirtieth."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"What follows is the comparison that decides between them: wxG and xG‚òÖ, a fitted blend of the four xG variants, against the plain xG model that sits inside both. What survives on the site today is the plainest thing on this page ‚Äî not for lack of alternatives, but because it is the one that wins the comparison."})]})}var eV=s(53549),eQ=s(80019),eZ=s(47643);let eY=[{value:"player",label:"Players"},{value:"team",label:"Teams"}],eX=[{value:"gf",label:"GF/60"},{value:"ga",label:"GA/60"},{value:"gf_pct",label:"G