rt's controls do",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Who"})," switches between skaters and teams. ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Predicting"})," chooses the target (GF/60, GA/60 or GF%), and every line moves with it because each metric is taken on the matching side. ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Strength"})," picks the bucket. ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Metric"})," switches between Pearson r, which is rank quality only and blind to a constant bias, and MAE, which is not."," ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Show"})," isolates the shot rates, the weighted-shots benchmark, or the four xG variants."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Read the team curves with more caution than the player curves: there are about thirty-two teams a season against nine hundred skaters, so every team point rests on a fraction of the sample. The caption under the chart reports the smallest sample behind the view you are looking at."})]})]})}function e4(e){let{head:t,lead:s,children:n}=e;return(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("details",{children:[(0,a.jsxs)("summary",{className:"cursor-pointer",children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:t})," ",s]}),n?(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"mt-2 space-y-2",children:n}):null]})})}function e6(){let e=eF.offence[eE.flagship],t=e=>eP.find(t=>t[0]===e),s=t("5v5 / defence"),n=t("pk / defence");return(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{id:"limitations",title:"Limitations and open questions",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-ink-faint italic",children:"Tap any line for the fuller version."}),(0,a.jsxs)("ul",{className:"list-disc ml-6 space-y-3 text-ink-soft",children:[(0,a.jsx)(e4,{head:"The ceiling is low, and that is the question's doing.",lead:(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:["Even the best cell, 5v5 offence at r = ",eO(e),", explains about ",eO(e*e)," of the variance in a skater's future on-ice goal rate."]}),children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Every defensive and special-teams cell does worse. On-ice goal rates are noisy and team-contaminated, so at this signal level no amount of feature engineering separates cleanly from any other. If the number is to move, it moves through a better target or more data, not better weighting."})}),(0,a.jsx)(e4,{head:"The xG rows are a little optimistic.",lead:"The talent walker's settings were tuned with the test seasons in view.",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Its variances were re-tuned over all seasons, including the seasons the held-out test uses, and nobody has measured by how much. Corsi and Tango have nothing fitted. The numbers also predate the 2026-09 xG refit and will be re-measured after it."})}),(0,a.jsx)(e4,{head:"The shot model reads the power play worse.",lead:"Ranking shots by danger is harder with a man advantage.",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Location and shot type carry most of the signal we have, and on the power play they carry less of it. A point shot tipped in traffic and a point shot into shins look nearly identical in the data, and the things that tell them apart (seam passes, screens, one-timers off cross-ice feeds) are not in the play-by-play. That is a gap in the model independent of anything above."})}),(0,a.jsx)(e4,{head:"On the penalty kill, shot quality and shot volume cannot be told apart.",lead:(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:[eG[eE.flagship]," scores ",eD(eM.raw.flagship)," against Corsi's"," ",eD(eM.raw.corsi),", with an interval that spans zero."]}),children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Scored with the goalie removed, it is a three-way tie between Tango (",eD(eM.neutral.tango),"), ",eG[eE.flagship]," (",eD(eM.neutral.flagship),") and Corsi (",eD(eM.neutral.corsi),"). We do not claim shot quality beats shot volume on the penalty kill, and we do not claim it loses. This cell cannot currently tell the two apart."]})}),(0,a.jsx)(e4,{head:"Shot quality is less stable than shot volume on defence.",lead:(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:[eG[eE.flagship]," repeats at ",eO(s[4])," at 5v5 and ",eO(n[4])," on the penalty kill, against Corsi's ",eO(s[1])," and ",eO(n[1]),"."]}),children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"That is a real trade. At 5v5 it predicts better than Corsi does but wobbles more from one half-season to the next, because it is a sum over continuous per-shot values rather than a count. For ranking players over a full season the predictivity matters more. Over ten games, treat the defensive numbers with more caution than the offensive ones."})}),(0,a.jsx)(e4,{head:"A skater's xGF is partly his linemates' finishing.",lead:"Shooter adjustment is applied per shot, to whoever took it.",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"On-ice xGF aggregates a whole line, so a finisher who outperforms the base model lifts the on-ice xGF of every teammate who was out there, including a playmaker who touched the puck and never shot it. Some of what reads as shot creation here is a linemate riding along."})}),(0,a.jsx)(e4,{head:"The rolling xG model relearns its own coordinate-to-goal mapping.",lead:"That keeps it calibrated to the tracking system, and also bakes the system's errors in.",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The base model is refit on a rolling window of recent shots, so it reflects the current relationship between reported shot location and goals. Any systematic error in how this era's tracking reports location becomes signal, not something corrected out. xG here is the probability of a goal given ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"reported"})," location, which in this era is not guaranteed to be true shot danger."]})}),(0,a.jsx)(e4,{head:"Everything here rests on the underlying xG model.",lead:"This page does not validate it.",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["xG(sh) is built entirely from that model's outputs, so if the base model is miscalibrated xG(sh) inherits the error in full. The"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"?tab=writeups&writeup=xg",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"xG writeup"})," ","checks the model against other public xG models and states its own caveats, which apply here too."]})}),(0,a.jsx)(e4,{head:"Three seasons, about 110 test players per cell.",lead:"Most comparisons on this page carry an interval that spans zero.",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The 5v5 offence result is the one exception. We have tried to say ‚Äúwe could not show a difference‚Äù rather than ‚Äúthere is no difference,‚Äù but the distinction is easy to lose and worth holding onto."})})]})]})}let e8=[["finding","What each layer buys"],["chart","Predictivity vs sample size"],["limitations","Limitations and open questions"],["appendix","Appendix: the tables and the history"]];function e9(){return(0,a.jsxs)("article",{id:"weighted-shots-writeup",className:"prose-paper",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("header",{className:"mb-10",children:[(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"dateline",children:"Writeups \xb7 Shot metrics"}),(0,a.jsx)("h1",{className:"font-serif text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-2 mb-3",children:"Back to plain xG"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"deck",children:"Tom Tango proposed weighted shots in 2014. We tested every layer built on shooter-adjusted xG since, and none beats it. What survives is the plainest thing in the stack: expected goals, shooter adjusted for, goalie kept out."}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Byline,{})]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Toc,{items:e8}),(0,a.jsx)(eq,{}),(0,a.jsx)(e5,{}),(0,a.jsx)(e6,{}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Appendix,{title:"The tables and the history",intro:"Reference material. Each drawer opens on its own.",children:[(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"The held-out tables, the goalie fork and repeatability",children:(0,a.jsx)(eK,{})}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"A brief history of weighted shots",children:(0,a.jsx)(eU,{})})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"text-sm text-ink-soft mt-10 border-t border-rule pt-4",children:["Two metrics came out of this line of work and are documented, not published: xG‚òÖ, a fitted blend of the xG variants, and wxG, a weighted blend built on top of it. Both are covered in the"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"?tab=writeups&writeup=archive",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"Archive"}),", along with the evidence against them."]})]})}function e7(){return(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"Overview",id:"xg-overview",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Expected Goals (xG) assigns a goal probability to every unblocked shot attempt (Fenwick shot) based on the shot's geometry and context ‚Äî before anyone scores. The number answers: ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"given where and how this shot was taken, what fraction of identical shots go in?"})]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The model produces four variants per shot, not one. Each strips out a different piece of observed skill:"}),(0,a.jsxs)("table",{className:"w-full text-sm mt-2 mb-3 border border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("thead",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule-strong text-ink-faint text-[11px] tracking-wider",children:[(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-left font-medium",children:"Column"}),(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-left font-medium",children:"What it controls for"}),(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-left font-medium",children:"Primary use"})]})}),(0,a.jsxs)("tbody",{children:[(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs",children:"xg_unadjusted"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2",children:"Shot geometry + context only"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2",children:"Closest to public xG; shot-quality baselines"})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs",children:"xg_shooter_adj"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2",children:"Adds shooter talent posterior"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2",children:"Goalie evaluation (removes shooter skill)"})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs",children:"xg_goalie_adj"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2",children:"Adds goalie-and-context talent posterior"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2",children:"Shooter evaluation (removes goalie-and-context skill)"})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs",children:"xg_fully_adj"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2",children:"Both shooter + goalie posteriors"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2",children:"Overachievement vs a player's own established skill"})]})]})]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Formula,{lines:["xg_unadjusted  = sigmoid(base_logit + E)","xg_shooter_adj = sigmoid(base_logit + E + Œº_shooter)","xg_goalie_adj  = sigmoid(base_logit + E ‚àí Œº_goalie)","xg_fully_adj   = sigmoid(base_logit + E + Œº_shooter ‚àí Œº_goalie)"]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Four formulas, one model. ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"base_logit"})," is a single fitted shot-quality regression, and ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"Œº_shooter"})," and"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"Œº_goalie"})," are the same two numbers from a single talent filter; the columns differ only in which of those terms is added before the sigmoid. Nothing is refit per column, which is what makes the four comparable on identical shots."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"E"}),' is the league scoring environment ‚Äî one number per season, learned sequentially as that season\'s shots arrive ‚Äî and it sits under all four columns alike. The column names describe talent adjustments only; "unadjusted" means no shooter or goalie adjustment, not no environment. That matters because raw xG is shot quality for an ',(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"average"})," shooter against an average goalie in that season's environment, and average shooters do not take power-play shots ‚Äî raw alone runs a few percent under on the man advantage, and the shooter-adjusted variant is what corrects for it."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The reason for four columns is that different questions need different baselines, and the rule for choosing is: ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"whose personnel does this adjustment encode, and do they persist?"})," Shooter adjustment on the for-side encodes your own shooters; goalie adjustment on the against-side encodes your own goalie; the opposite sides encode your opponents, which is mostly schedule. For descriptive work, include your own personnel and exclude your opponents'. For forecasting, include the personnel that persist ‚Äî goalies do, skaters turn over. Evaluating a goalie requires a baseline that does ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"not"})," contain that goalie, which is ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"xg_shooter_adj"}),"; evaluating a shooter requires one that does not contain that shooter, which is"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"xg_goalie_adj"}),". Measuring how far a player is beating their own established skill needs a baseline containing both, which is"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"xg_fully_adj"}),"."]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-6 mb-2",children:"Where xG flows downstream"}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Every downstream metric in the system pulls from these columns. Aggregation rolls them up per (player, game, strength state) into the ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"xgf_*"})," / ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"xga_*"})," / ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"ixg_*"})," columns that appear in the leaderboard. Net Rating takes the on-ice"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"xgf_shooter_adj"})," and ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"xga_shooter_adj"})," sums ‚Äî the same headline pairing the site leads with, deliberately not the fully-adjusted columns: goalie adjustment incorporates the team's own goaltending into the defensive half of a skater metric rather than neutralising it, which is not what a play-driving rating should measure. RAPM uses the same columns at shift grain. The GSAx goalie metric is ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"xg_shooter_adj ‚àí actual_goal"})," per shot, summed over a goalie's games ‚Äî a baseline that excludes the goalie being judged."]})]})}function te(){return(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"Pipeline",id:"xg-pipeline",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The xG suite is a four-stage chain. The first two stages run offline, once per model refit; the last two run chronologically on every new game."}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Formula,{lines:["Every unblocked shot attempt","   ‚îÇ","   ‚îÇ  FEATURE BUILD ‚Äî geometry, game context, categorical encodings","   ‚ñº","BASE MODEL FIT  (offline, once per refit)","   ‚îÇ  penalised logistic regression, one ridge per coefficient block,","   ‚îÇ  fit on an earlier slice of seasons and tested on a later one,","   ‚îÇ  with the pre-shot shooter-minus-goalie talent difference entered","   ‚îÇ  as a fixed, unpenalised offset ‚Äî so the coefficients stop","   ‚îÇ  absorbing the average talent of whoever happened to be shooting","   ‚îÇ  ‚Üí coefficients + calibration metadata","   ‚ñº","CHRONOLOGICAL SCORER  (every shot, in time order)","   ‚îÇ  read each player's talent estimate and the league environment","   ‚îÇ  as they stood before the shot, score the shot, then update","   ‚îÇ  shooter, goalie and environment together with the outcome","   ‚îÇ  ‚Üí four values per shot:","   ‚îÇ      unadjusted \xb7 shooter-adjusted \xb7 goalie-adjusted \xb7 fully-adjusted","   ‚ñº","AGGREGATION","   ‚îÇ  rolled up per player, per game, per strength state","   ‚îî‚Üí  xGF / xGA / individual xG"]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The chronological order in stage three is the constraint that makes the rest trustworthy. Each shot's talent estimates are read"," ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"before"})," that shot is processed, and only updated afterward. No shot can be scored using information about how it, or anything after it, turned out ‚Äî the model never sees the result it is predicting."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The base model is fit offline and its coefficients frozen to a stored artifact. Scoring reads that artifact; the two stages never run together, so a refit in progress cannot contaminate live numbers. Because the fitter takes the pre-shot talent difference as a fixed offset rather than leaving it for the coefficients to soak up, the base model's own strength-state intercepts carry no passenger: a power-play shot is not coded as inherently more dangerous merely because power-play shooters are better than average ‚Äî that talent is credited to the shooter term, leaving the base geometry to describe the shot itself."}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The fitter is a hand-rolled penalised logistic regression rather than an off-the-shelf one, for a specific reason: it applies a"," ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"separate"})," penalty strength per block of coefficients instead of one global setting. That is what lets the strength-state geometry terms be shrunk hard toward the 5v5 baseline ‚Äî where the data is thin, the model should fall back on what it knows ‚Äî while the main effects stay free. A single global penalty cannot express that, and at this sample size a single global penalty would in any case do essentially nothing: roughly 300,000 training shots outweigh the penalty term by the same factor, which leaves the regularisation decorative rather than real."]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-6 mb-2",id:"xg-rink-adjustment",children:"Rink adjustment"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"For most of the archive, shot coordinates were logged by each arena's own scorer, and every scorer distorts distance and lateral position a little differently. The base model reads those coordinates as given, so the distortion lands directly in xG. It is also persistent: a goalie plays half his games in one building, so he and his backups share it, and it is the kind of error a goalie rating cannot tell from talent."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The correction works arena by arena and season by season. Visiting teams' shot distances at an arena are lined up, quantile for quantile, against the league's road distances for the same season, which gives a smooth map from what that arena recorded to what the league would have recorded. Visitors are the yardstick because a team's road schedule is close to a random sample of the league, so shooting style cancels and the arena is what is left. The map is applied to every event at that arena, home and away, shots and non-shots alike, before any geometry is computed, so every feature reads one consistent coordinate system."}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Scoring a game may only use what was known before it, so an arena's map is built from its own earlier games, which in October means almost nothing. Arena bias carries from one year to the next (a correlation of ","0.755","), so each arena-season starts from its own previous map and moves toward the current evidence as it accumulates. An arena with fewer than"," ",5e3.toLocaleString()," visiting shots in a season is not mapped."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"It stops after 2021-22, and that was measured."})," Through 2021-22 the gap between an arena's own team at home and on the road tracks the visitors' bias there (a slope of"," ","0.70","), the signature of a scorer mislocating everybody. From 2022-23 the slope falls to about"," ","0.29",": what is left is mostly the home team genuinely suppressing its opponents' chances, and adjusting that away would remove real hockey. The spread of the bias across arenas drops from ","1.71"," ft in 2021-22 to ","1.21"," ft in 2022-23, and on 2022-23 the adjustment made calibration worse."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"What it buys is fairness across arenas, not accuracy."})," In 2021-22, the last season it applies to, it cut the spread between arenas from ","14.31"," to ","7.80"," percentage points while log loss did not move."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Two limits."})," The map that can be used in scoring, built without looking ahead, is weaker than a full-season one: it removes some of an arena's bias, not all of it. And the measurement study covers 2012-13 onward, so 2010-11 and 2011-12 are adjusted on the strength of the later seasons and were never checked directly."]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-6 mb-2",children:"Re-run policy"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"A refit is triggered manually when a meaningful volume of new seasons has accumulated. It produces two sets of coefficients, not one: a chip-era model for 2023-24 onward, and a pre-chip model for 2010-11 through 2022-23. Within that pre-chip span, seasons through 2021-22 have their coordinates corrected for the recording arena's own scorer before fitting; 2022-23 is scored as recorded, because by then what separates one arena from another is mostly real home-ice effect rather than scorer error. Scoring and aggregation then run automatically after each scrape cycle. A refit forces a re-score of every shot in the archive, which takes about forty minutes; an ordinary night only scores the games that were played, continuing the talent filter from where it stopped rather than starting it over."})]})}let tt=[{num:1,name:"dist_to_net",category:"Geometric (5v5 baseline)",notes:"Euclidean feet to nearest net post (NHL coords; arena-adjusted through 2021-22)"},{num:2,name:"shot_angle",category:"Geometric (5v5 baseline)",notes:"Signed in x, over the full half-turn: 0 = straight-on, œÄ/2 = on the goal line, past œÄ/2 = behind the net, œÄ = directly behind it (same arena adjustment as distance)"},{num:3,name:"dist_x_angle",category:"Geometric (5v5 baseline)",notes:"Interaction term"},{num:4,name:"tp_*",category:"Geometric (5v5 baseline)",notes:"Tensor product of natural-cubic-spline bases in distance and angle ‚Äî 26 columns that let danger be a curved surface over the two rather than a straight line in each. The three terms above are its linear corner, so the log-linear model is nested inside it. The angle margin carries a knot past œÄ/2 so the surface keeps bending as it wraps behind the net."},{num:5,name:"strength_geometry_*",category:"Strength geometry",notes:"Per-state deviation of dist_to_net/shot_angle/dist_x_angle from the 5v5 baseline, ridged toward zero (DEVIATION_RIDGE=100)"},{num:6,name:"strength_all_*",category:"Strength",notes:"One dummy per skater-pair state: 5v5, 5v4, 5v3, 4v3, 4v5, 6v5, 6v4, 5v6, 4v4, 3v3, other"},{num:7,name:"is_home",category:"Contextual",notes:"Shooter on home team"},{num:8,name:"is_rebound",category:"Contextual",notes:"Prior Fenwick by same team within 3s, same period"},{num:9,name:"own_net_empty",category:"Contextual",notes:"Shooting team's own goalie pulled ‚Äî still a live situation, since the shooter's own net being empty does not change what they are shooting at"},{num:10,name:"crossed_royal_road",category:"Geometric",notes:"Puck crossed the centre line between the faceoff dots within 1-3s before the shot, forcing the goalie to move laterally across the crease. The third most valuable feature in the model."},{num:11,name:"time_since_prev_event",category:"Prior event",notes:"Seconds since previous on-ice play event (capped 30s)"},{num:12,name:"dist_from_prev_event",category:"Prior event",notes:"Feet from previous on-ice play event location (capped 200ft)"},{num:13,name:"prev_event_same_team",category:"Prior event",notes:"Previous event was by the shooting team"},{num:14,name:"prev_event_faceoff",category:"Prior event",notes:"Faceoff just won ‚Üí shot from the dot"},{num:15,name:"prev_event_hit",category:"Prior event",notes:"Just got/gave a hit; possession compromised"},{num:16,name:"prev_event_giveaway",category:"Prior event",notes:"Loose-puck recovery in unusual spot"},{num:17,name:"prev_event_takeaway",category:"Prior event",notes:""},{num:18,name:"prev_event_blocked-shot",category:"Prior event",notes:"Chaotic post-block recovery"},{num:19,name:"prev_event_missed-shot",category:"Prior event",notes:""},{num:20,name:"prev_event_shot-on-goal",category:"Prior event",notes:"Non-rebound trailing shot"},{num:21,name:"shot_type_wrist",category:"Categorical",notes:""},{num:22,name:"shot_type_snap",category:"Categorical",notes:""},{num:23,name:"shot_type_slap",category:"Categorical",notes:""},{num:24,name:"shot_type_backhand",category:"Categorical",notes:""},{num:25,name:"shot_type_tip-in",category:"Categorical",notes:"Negative relative to wrist at same distance"},{num:26,name:"shot_type_deflected",category:"Categorical",notes:""},{num:27,name:"shot_type_wrap-around",category:"Categorical",notes:""},{num:28,name:"shot_type_poke",category:"Categorical",notes:"1,300+ samples, distinct goal rate ‚Äî kept separate from 'other'"},{num:29,name:"shot_type_bat",category:"Categorical",notes:"Same ‚Äî 11‚Äì13% goal rate, high enough for own one-hot"}];function ts(){return(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"Features",id:"xg-features",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Built by ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"xg_features.build_feature_matrix"}),". The full design matrix runs to 93 features; the table below groups them by block rather than listing all 93 individually. Coefficient values are not reproduced here ‚Äî the fitted vector lives in the stored model artifact."]}),(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"overflow-x-auto my-4",children:(0,a.jsxs)("table",{className:"w-full text-sm border border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("thead",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule-strong text-ink-faint text-[11px] tracking-wider",children:[(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right font-medium w-8",children:"#"}),(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-left font-medium",children:"Feature"}),(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-left font-medium",children:"Category"}),(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-left font-medium",children:"Notes"})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("tbody",{children:tt.map(e=>(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule last:border-b-0",children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right tabular-nums text-ink-faint",children:e.num}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-1.5 font-mono text-xs",children:e.name}),(0,a.jsx)(