{children:["The refit produces two separate penalised logistic regression models ‚Äî each carrying its own penalty strength per block of coefficients rather than one global setting for the whole model: a ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"chip-era"})," ","model trained on 2023-24 onward, and a ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"pre-chip"})," model trained on 2010-11 through 2022-23. Each shot is scored against the model from its own era. Splitting the fit isolates the coordinate-precision regime change at the 2023-10-10 chip-tracking rollout ‚Äî pre-chip rink-recorded x/y were noisier, so geometry coefficients fit on chip-era data don't transfer cleanly backward."]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-6 mb-2",children:"Temporal train/test split"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Each model splits temporally, not randomly. The chip-era model trains on 2023-24 + 2024-25 and tests on the in-progress 2025-26 season. The pre-chip model trains on 2010-11 through 2021-22 and tests on 2022-23. Both surface forward-in-time generalization rather than the in-sample leakage that k-fold cross-validation would allow on time-series shot data."}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-6 mb-2",children:"Diagnostics (chip-era model)"}),(0,a.jsxs)("table",{className:"w-full text-sm mt-2 mb-3 border border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("thead",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule-strong text-ink-faint text-[11px] tracking-wider",children:[(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-left font-medium",children:"Metric"}),(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right font-medium",children:"Train"}),(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right font-medium",children:"Test"})]})}),(0,a.jsxs)("tbody",{children:[(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2",children:"Log-loss"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right tabular-nums",children:"0.21798"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right tabular-nums",children:"0.22583"})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2",children:"Brier score"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right tabular-nums",children:"0.05882"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right tabular-nums",children:"0.06099"})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2",children:"ROC AUC"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right tabular-nums",children:"0.75964"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right tabular-nums",children:"0.74547"})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2",children:"ECE (10-bin)"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right tabular-nums text-ink-faint",children:"‚Äî"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right tabular-nums",children:"0.00527"})]})]})]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Train sits at 237,826 Fenwick shots against a 6.77% goal rate; test at 116,222 shots. The train/test gap is small for log-loss (+0.008) and Brier (+0.002), indicating the model generalizes without overfitting. Test ECE of 0.00527 says predicted probabilities match empirical goal rates closely across the probability range. See the Calibration section for the per-strength breakdown. These figures are for the static chip-era fit; the rolling model that actually scores shots refits every 250 games and at every season boundary across 18 segments on a 1,300-game window, with mean absolute segment bias of 3.00%."}),(0,a.jsxs)("div",{className:"border-l-2 border-accent pl-4 my-6 text-sm",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"mb-2",children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Why AUC is not comparable across populations."})," ","Empty-net shots convert at about 55% against roughly 6.8% for everything else ‚Äî trivially easy to separate from a genuine scoring chance, which inflates AUC without reflecting how well the model reads shot quality against a goaltender. They are excluded from the model entirely, so this AUC describes only the hard part of the problem: shots a goalie actually has to stop."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The comparison that isolates the effect of that exclusion is a controlled one: fit two models on identical games, one including empty-net shots and one without, then score both on the same goalie-in-only test set. On that matched population, excluding empty-net shots does not cost accuracy ‚Äî AUC moves by"," ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"+0.00024"}),", with log-loss, Brier and ECE all moving the same direction. AUC is only meaningful when the scored population is held fixed, which is also why the chip-era and pre-chip models are always reported and compared within their own era rather than against each other."]})]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-6 mb-2",children:"Where the danger is"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Distance and angle do not enter as two straight lines. Danger is a surface over both at once: the odds collapse over the first fifteen feet and flatten past forty, and how steeply they fall depends on the angle, so a shot from thirty feet in the middle of the ice is a different shot from one thirty feet out on the goal line. The model estimates that surface with a tensor-product spline ‚Äî a smooth two-dimensional basis in distance and angle, fitted jointly rather than one curve per axis ‚Äî which is why the contours below bend around the crease instead of running parallel to it."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The angle is measured as a half-turn, not a quarter-turn: zero straight on, a right angle on the goal line, and beyond that, behind the net. That sounds like bookkeeping and is not. Measured as a quarter-turn ‚Äî which is how this model read the ice until September 2026 ‚Äî a wraparound from five feet behind the goal line and a one-timer from five feet in front of it are the same distance and the same angle, so they are the same row of the design matrix and get the same number. They are not the same shot. Over the chip era, attempts from behind the line convert at 3.9% against 11.8% for their distance-matched mirror in front. Signing the angle is what lets the surface tell them apart."}),(0,a.jsx)(tf,{}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-6 mb-2",children:"Model structure"}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The model uses 93 features, 26 of them the spline surface above. There is no 5v5 interaction column, so the main-effect geometry coefficients (",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"dist_to_net"}),","," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"shot_angle"}),", ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"dist_x_angle"}),") ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"are"})," the 5v5 geometry, and every other strength state (5v4, 5v3, 4v3, 4v5, 6v5, 6v4, 5v6, 4v4, 3v3, other) carries a coefficient block for its ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"deviation"})," from that 5v5 baseline, ridged toward zero. Goalie status (",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"own_net_empty"}),","," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"own_net_empty"}),") is carried as its own feature rather than folded into the strength categories, so an empty net composes with whatever skater state it occurs in instead of multiplying out the category count. See Features for the full list and Ablation for what each block is worth. Every fitted value, with units, is published in"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"?tab=writeups&writeup=coefficients#coefficients-writeup",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"the coefficients writeup"}),"."]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-6 mb-2",children:"Pre-chip model"}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The pre-chip model shares the same penalised fitting approach and produces coefficients with matching signs and ordering to the chip-era model ‚Äî slap and snap shots rank highest, wrap-arounds and tip-ins lowest, distance and angle penalize scoring in the expected direction. Coefficients differ in magnitude where coordinate noise washes out the geometric signal:",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"dist_to_net"})," and ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"shot_angle"})," coefficients sit modestly closer to zero, because the pre-chip x/y are recorded by rink scorers rather than puck-tracking chips, and that noise dilutes the per-foot and per-radian effect. The contextual block is essentially unaffected, since those features come from event metadata, not from coordinates."]})]})}let tx={empty_net:"Empty net",other:"Other"};function tb(e){let{cx:t,cy:s,payload:n}=e;if(void 0===t||void 0===s||!n)return null;let o=Math.max(3,Math.sqrt(n.n/10842*400));return(0,a.jsx)("circle",{cx:t,cy:s,r:o,fill:"#3b82f6",fillOpacity:.7,stroke:"#1d4ed8",strokeWidth:1})}function ty(){var e;let[t,s]=(0,n.useState)(null),[o,r]=(0,n.useState)(!0);return(0,n.useEffect)(()=>{(0,eV.W)("xg/calibration.json").then(s).catch(()=>s(null)).finally(()=>r(!1))},[]),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"Calibration",id:"xg-calibration",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"A calibrated probability model should have predicted probability equal to empirical goal rate in each bin. The chart below plots mean predicted xG (x-axis) against actual goal rate (y-axis) for ten equal-size bins. Dots scaled by bin population. The diagonal is perfect calibration."}),o?(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-ink-faint",children:"Loading‚Ä¶"}):t?(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:[(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"w-full h-[380px] mt-4",children:(0,a.jsx)(eo.u,{width:"100%",height:"100%",children:(0,a.jsxs)(em,{margin:{top:16,right:24,bottom:36,left:24},children:[(0,a.jsx)(ep.d,{strokeDasharray:"3 3"}),(0,a.jsx)(el.W,{dataKey:"mean_pred",type:"number",domain:[0,.3],name:"Mean predicted xG",tickFormatter:e=>(100*e).toFixed(0)+"%",label:{value:"Mean predicted xG",position:"insideBottom",offset:-20,fontSize:11}}),(0,a.jsx)(eh.h,{dataKey:"empirical_rate",type:"number",domain:[0,.3],name:"Empirical goal rate",tickFormatter:e=>(100*e).toFixed(0)+"%",label:{value:"Empirical goal rate",angle:-90,position:"insideLeft",offset:10,fontSize:11}}),(0,a.jsx)(eu.m,{formatte