rd-half validation (first half of each team's season, chronologically, predicting the second) on ",d.team_forward_half.offense.n," ","team-seasons, 2010-11 through 2025-26:"]}),(0,a.jsxs)("div",{className:"grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 mt-2 lg:grid-cols-2",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("div",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm font-medium text-ink-faint mb-1",children:"Offense: predicting next-half GF"}),(0,a.jsx)(tK,{bars:d.team_forward_half.offense.bars})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("div",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm font-medium text-ink-faint mb-1",children:"Defense: predicting next-half GA"}),(0,a.jsx)(tK,{bars:d.team_forward_half.defense.bars})]})]}),(0,a.jsx)(t0,{children:"Past goals is the weakest predictor of future goals on both sides of the puck; a bare shot-location model and MoneyPuck land within a point of each other; our talent-adjusted variants ‚Äî xG(sh) on offense, xGA(sv) on defense ‚Äî lead every one of them."}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-8 mb-2",children:"Shooter level: predicting next season"}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["This used to be one chart, and it was comparing two different questions on one podium. Raw sh% carries shot quality ‚Äî a player's shot mix (distance, angle, rush chances, one-timers off a specific power-play unit) persists from one season to the next, the same way linemates and deployment do. Our shooter talent is conversion ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"above"})," shot quality, by construction ‚Äî it has that stickiness engineered out. Racing the two on ‚Äúpredict next season's raw rate‚Äù always favored the metric that never tried to strip anything out. Two panels below instead, on the same ",d.shooter_next_season.n_player_seasons," ","shooter-seasons: at least ",d.shooter_next_season.min_shots," Fenwick shots (",d.shooter_next_season.season_scope,") in both years,"," ",tB(d.shooter_next_season.season_range[0])," through"," ",tB(d.shooter_next_season.season_range[1]),", empty net excluded."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"font-serif text-base mt-5 mb-1",children:["B1 ‚Äî ",d.shooter_next_season.b1.title]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Four ways to read this season's rate, apples to apples ‚Äî none of the four has shot quality netted out: raw sh%; a shot-multiplier ‚Äúregressed‚Äù sh% (goals plus"," ",d.shooter_next_season.b1.regression_constant.chosen," ","league-average shots, divided by shots plus that same constant); location-only xG per shot (shot quality alone, the same read a public shot map gives you); and our xG(sh) per shot."]}),(0,a.jsx)(tK,{bars:d.shooter_next_season.b1.bars,height:170}),(0,a.jsxs)(t0,{children:["Pearson r against next season's actual goals/shot. Our xG(sh) per shot wins this panel outright (",null==(e=tX(d.shooter_next_season.b1.bars,"our_sh"))?void 0:e.r.toFixed(3)," ","vs. ",null==(t=tX(d.shooter_next_season.b1.bars,"raw_shpct"))?void 0:t.r.toFixed(3)," ","for raw sh%) ‚Äî but every entry here is within shouting distance of the others, because all four are still reading shot mix along with everything else. That's expected, not a loophole: none of these four predictors claims to isolate finishing skill."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"font-serif text-base mt-4 mb-2",children:"Our xG(sh) per shot vs. next season's rate, with the fitted line:"}),(0,a.jsx)(tU,{scatter:d.shooter_next_season.b1.scatter,fit:d.shooter_next_season.b1.fit,xLabel:d.shooter_next_season.b1.scatter_x_label,yLabel:d.shooter_next_season.b1.scatter_y_label,seriesName:"Player-season"}),(0,a.jsx)(t0,{children:"Points decimated for file size; the correlation above is computed on the full sample."}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"font-serif text-base mt-6 mb-1",children:["B2 ‚Äî ",d.shooter_next_season.b2.title]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The question raw sh% was never built to answer: not who scores the most, but who finishes above what their shots were actually worth. Target is next season's log(goals / location-only xG) ‚Äî positive means outscoring shot quality, negative means underscoring it. Predictors: goals minus xG per shot (the usual additive ‚Äúfinishing above expected‚Äù read); the raw log ratio this season; a regressed version of that ratio (the same shot-multiplier idea applied to the raw goal rate first, this time fit against THIS target ‚Äî ",d.shooter_next_season.b2.regression_constant.chosen," ","league-average shots); and our shooter-talent posterior."]}),(0,a.jsx)(tK,{bars:d.shooter_next_season.b2.bars,height:170}),(0,a.jsxs)(t0,{children:["Our talent read wins this panel outright (",null==(s=tX(d.shooter_next_season.b2.bars,"talent"))?void 0:s.r.toFixed(3),") ‚Äî the one question it was actually built to answer. Once ‚Äúfinishing above expected‚Äù is the target, raw sh% isn't even in this race; the closest conventional read (regressed log-ratio) still trails by a real margin."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"font-serif text-base mt-4 mb-2",children:"Our shooter talent vs. next season's finishing above expected, with the fitted line:"}),(0,a.jsx)(tU,{scatter:d.shooter_next_season.b2.scatter,fit:d.shooter_next_season.b2.fit,xLabel:d.shooter_next_season.b2.scatter_x_label,yLabel:d.shooter_next_season.b2.scatter_y_label}),(0,a.jsxs)(t0,{children:["Calibration slope ",d.shooter_next_season.b2.fit.slope.toFixed(3)," ‚Äî positive and the right sign, still noisy at this grain. Points decimated for file size; the correlation above is computed on the full sample. The stability chart below is the other half of this story: judged on whether the SAME metric repeats itself, rather than on a noisy single-season target, our talent read is not close."]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-8 mb-2",children:"Goalie level: predicting next season"}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The same B1/B2 split, mirrored for goalies, on"," ",d.goalie_next_season.n_goalie_seasons," goalie-seasons with at least ",d.goalie_next_season.min_shots," Fenwick shots faced in both years (",d.goalie_next_season.season_scope,"),"," ",tB(d.goalie_next_season.seaso