ix) ‚Äî the same pattern as the shooter side. Once the target is defined net of shot quality, the metric built to separate skill from shot quality is the one that predicts it, even on a panel (C1, above) where a big enough shrinkage constant beats it on the raw rate itself."]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-8 mb-2",children:"Who the raw read flatters ‚Äî and who it buries"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"A GSAx-style evaluation ‚Äî goals against subtracted from a location-only xG against ‚Äî is only honest if it grades the same regardless of how long a goalie has been in the league. It doesn't. Goals divided by raw xG, bucketed by career shots faced or taken and pooled across all sixteen seasons:"}),(0,a.jsxs)("div",{className:"grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 mt-2 lg:grid-cols-2",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("div",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm font-medium text-ink-faint mb-1",children:"Goalies, by career shots faced"}),(0,a.jsx)(tV,{buckets:d.flattered.goalie.buckets,raw:d.flattered.goalie.series[d.flattered.goalie.raw_key],ours:d.flattered.goalie.series[d.flattered.goalie.ours_key],rawLabel:d.flattered.goalie.raw_label,oursLabel:d.flattered.goalie.ours_label,nShots:d.flattered.goalie.n_shots})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("div",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm font-medium text-ink-faint mb-1",children:"Shooters, by career shots taken"}),(0,a.jsx)(tV,{buckets:d.flattered.shooter.buckets,raw:d.flattered.shooter.series[d.flattered.shooter.raw_key],ours:d.flattered.shooter.series[d.flattered.shooter.ours_key],rawLabel:d.flattered.shooter.raw_label,oursLabel:d.flattered.shooter.ours_label,nShots:d.flattered.shooter.n_shots})]})]}),(0,a.jsx)(t0,{children:"1.00 is the honest line. Raw xG runs hot on callup goalies and cold on debutant shooters, then drifts the other way by the veteran end of each career curve; our adjusted variants sit closer to flat across the whole range ‚Äî most visibly for shooters, where the raw spread (0.95 to 1.06) roughly compresses to a tenth of that (0.98 to 1.00) once shooter talent is priced in."}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-8 mb-2",children:"Era: comparing seasons without an era term"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"A conventional read compares raw rates across seasons directly ‚Äî there is no era term to consult. League goals per Fenwick shot, no model, next to our environment term E (season end, logit scale, from the Environment section above):"}),(0,a.jsx)(tQ,{bySeason:d.era.by_season,chipBoundary:d.era.chip_boundary}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Leaguewide scoring moved"," ",(100*d.era.illustration.without_adjustment.relative_change).toFixed(1),"% from ",tB(d.era.illustration.season_a)," (",(100*d.era.illustration.without_adjustment.a).toFixed(2),"%) to"," ",tB(d.era.illustration.season_b)," (",(100*d.era.illustration.without_adjustment.b).toFixed(2),"%) ‚Äî a gap a raw-rate comparison has no way to explain away. Nothing about any individual shooter or goalie changed the league that much; it's exactly the kind of shift E exists to absorb, booked to its own term each season instead of leaking into whoever happened to be on the ice while it happened. That's also why a player's talent posterior is portable across seasons ‚Äî including across the 2023-10-10 chip-era boundary, where the base shot model itself changes ‚Äî while E is not; see Roman Josi and James Reimer below for what that portability looks like on an actual career."]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-8 mb-2",children:"Stability: does the metric agree with itself next year?"}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["A different question from prediction above: not whether this season's number predicts next season's outcome, but whether it predicts next season's ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"same measurement"})," ‚Äî year-over-year Pearson r of raw, regressed, and our talent read, recomputed per season pair."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("div",{className:"grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 mt-2 lg:grid-cols-2",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("div",{children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"text-sm font-medium text-ink-faint mb-1",children:["Shooters (‚â•",d.stability.shooters.min_shots," shots both seasons)"]}),(0,a.jsx)(tZ,{pairs:d.stability.shooters.pairs})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("div",{children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"text-sm font-medium text-ink-faint mb-1",children:["Goalies (‚â•",d.stability.goalies.min_shots," shots faced both seasons)"]}),(0,a.jsx)(tZ,{pairs:d.stability.goalies.pairs,showGsax:!0})]})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(t0,{children:["Our talent read is the most stable line in both panels, every single season pair, by a wide margin ‚Äî goalies especially: raw save rate and GSAx wander close to zero (occasionally negative) while talent holds 0.58 and up. The shot-multiplier fix barely moves the needle for shooters and needed a very large constant (",d.stability.goalies.regression_constant.toLocaleString()," ","shots' worth of league rate) to help goalies at all ‚Äî a single goalie-season is that noisy on its own."]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-8 mb-2",children:"Six players"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The aggregates above are the honest case. Here it is on actual players ‚Äî three shooters, three goalies, each picked by a fixed rule against the current data, not cherry-picked for story. Lines share one axis: talent is converted to an implied rate the same way the model itself builds a probability ‚Äî anchored at the leaguewide baseline, with the player's own posterior applied on top ‚Äî so it sits next to raw and regressed rate honestly rather than as a same-shaped-but-different-scale ratio."}),(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"grid grid-cols-1 gap-8 mt-4 lg:grid-cols-2",children:[...d.examples.shooters,...d.examples.goalies].map(e=>(0,a.jsxs)("div",{children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"font-serif text-base mb-1",children:[e.name," ",(0,a.jsxs)("span",{className:"text-ink-faint text-sm font-sans",children:["\xb7 ",tB(e.season),e.from_team&&e.to_team?" \xb7 ".concat(e.from_team," ‚Üí ").concat(e.to_team):""]})]}),(0,a.jsx)(tY,{trajectory:e.trajectory}),(0,a.jsx)(t0,{children:e.caption})]},e.role))})]}):(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-ink-faint",children:"Comparison data unavailable."})]})}function t1(){return(0,a.jsx)(h.Section,{title:"Known limitations",id:"xg-limitations",children:(0,a.jsxs)("ul",{className:"list-disc ml-6 space-y-3 my-3",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Calibration degrades with sample size, broadly."})," Per-strength ECE on the held-out test set is reported per strength state in the"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"#xg-calibration",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"Calibration"})," ","section, which renders it live from the fitted artifact rather than repeating it here. The pattern is consistent: 5v5 calibrates tightly because it has tens of thousands of test shots behind it, and every thin state is an order of magnitude worse. For the strength states that tracks sample size rather than model quality, and is probably not fixable by better features ‚Äî a few hundred shots a season cannot support well-calibrated probabilities no matter what goes into the model. Shot-level probabilities in the thin states deserve real skepticism; the impact on team-season aggregates is smaller, since those states contribute few shots in total."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Two distance bands still miss, and sample size does not explain either."})," They are about the same size. One is inside 10 ft: the model over-predicts 5‚Äì10 ft by about 3%. The miss is shared by rebounds and non-rebounds, sits in the same shots as the model‚Äôs top-decile over-prediction, and no distance or rebound term tested so far reaches it; it is open. The other is 30‚Äì35 ft, still under-predicted by about 5%. Beyond 22 ft the spline used to carry distance alone and missed by as much as 23%; six free-form far-distance bands fixed most of that, but the refit did not close the defencemen gap it was expected to. Defencemen‚Äôs shots still score about 2.4% more often than the model predicts (1.024, against 0.997 for forwards), so the oscillation was not its main cause. The table is in the"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"#xg-calibration",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"Calibration"})," ","section, rendered live from the fitted artifact."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"No goalie handedness."})," A glove-side vs. blocker-side effect is real ‚Äî goalies have structurally different save rates on their off-hand side. Shot-type one-hots partially proxy for this (wrap-arounds skew to one side), but there is no explicit handedness feature in the current design matrix."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"No QoC adjustment in xG itself."})," The base model treats all shots as coming from a league-average shooter and going toward a league-average goalie, and the EKF layer adjusts for the specific individuals involved. But the base probability does not account for the quality of opponents that created the shot opportunity, and building that in would create a chicken-and-egg dependency with the talent posteriors. Net Rating used to carry a QoC/QoT adjustment of its own; it was tested and retired in 2026-09 (see the Net Rating writeup) in favor of a narrower ice-time pace adjustment on expected chances against. Competition is still adjusted for elsewhere: the forecast behind xG impact and WP impact on the Shift Performance dashboard is keyed on the terciles of competition and teammate quality a shift was actually played against. It is the shot model, and now Net Rating, that leave it alone."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Pre-2022-23 coordinates are scorer-corrected; 2022-23 is not."})," The DB spans 2010-11 through today, and before puck tracking, x/y were logged by each arena's own rink scorer rather than a chip ‚Äî and every scorer carries a systematic distance and lateral bias (documented in earlier work by Post Hockey's founder), persistent enough that a goalie who plays half his games in one building would otherwise wear it as if it were talent. Seasons through 2021-22 correct for exactly that: each arena-season's visiting-team shot distribution is matched onto the league's road distribution, and the resulting map is applied to every shot at that rink, home and away alike, seeded from the arena's own prior season and shrunk toward nothing until the current season has evidence of its own. From 2022-23 the correction stops ‚Äî measured directly, what is left of the arena effect by then is mostly the home team's own chance suppression rather than scorer error, and adjusting it away would remove real hockey rather than noise. What noise the correction cannot reach, and all of it for 2022-23, is left for the shooter and goalie-and-context talent posteriors to absorb as wider uncertainty bands. Chip-era posteriors remain the authoritative source for current talent estimates; pre-chip posteriors are smoother but less precise per-shot. The pre-chip base model is fit walk-forward by season ‚Äî each season trained only on the pre-chip seasons before it, the same discipline the chip-era model uses ‚Äî rather than one static model fit on the whole era at once and scored in-sample. 2010-11 has no prior seasons to train on, so it is a bootstrap: fit on its own first slice of games and flagged as such in the model artifact, not a genuinely out-of-sample estimate the way every later pre-chip season is. 2023-24's move to chip tracking is a separate break from the scorer-correction question above ‚Äî a change in how much of the shot population gets recorded, not in where a recorded shot sits ‚Äî which is why the base model is still fit and scored on either side of it."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Shooter, goalie-and-context and league-wide talent are not separable from goals alone."})," If the league gets higher- or lower-scoring as a whole, goals cannot say whether that change belongs to shooters, to goalies, or to the game itself ‚Äî no method can recover that split from the outcome data alone. Rather than let that ambiguity land arbitrarily on shooter or goalie posteriors ‚Äî which would surface as a slow, un-anchored drift in the common level, with nothing holding it to a fixed point ‚Äî the model puts a named, published number ‚Äî"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"E"}),", the season environment ‚Äî in the place where the ambiguity actually lives, and holds shooter and goalie-and-context talent to a zero-sum, active-league frame instead. ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"E"})," is only comparable within an era: 2023-24's move to chip tracking is a measurement break, not a scoring change, so ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"E"})," should not be read across that boundary."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"The goalie term is goalie-and-context, not pure goaltending."})," ","It is estimated from the shots a goalie faces, so part of what it calls goalie talent is really the team in front of him (about half of it is shared by both of a team's goalies in a season), and in our out-of-sample tests the term added no measurable predictive value. It could optionally be shown shrunk toward league average ‚Äî about 0.4 of its raw deviation, the measured next-season carry-forward for goalies who stay on the same team ‚Äî though the site does not do this today; the posteriors shown elsewhere on the site are the raw, unshrunk version."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Posteriors carry across seasons."})," The current build runs a single chronological pass from 2010-11 to today ‚Äî talent posteriors carry across both season boundaries and the chip-era boundary with the same walk variance throughout. A transient walk-variance boost at 2023-10-10 was removed in October 2026 after it measured as a cost to out-of-sample accuracy; see the Bayesian section for the numbers."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"No shooter-position term, on purpose."})," At the same recorded distance, angle, shot type and situation, a defenceman's shot goes in more often than the model predicts beyond 30 feet and less often inside 20, and a forward's shot the reverse: the single distance curve is fitted to a pooled mix, and the two positions do not take the same shots from the same spots (a point shot through traffic in a set possession is not a forward's long throw on the rush). A defenceman indicator in the base model improved out-of-sample forecasting by a small, measured amount (about 0.00002 of log loss per shot, a tenth of what shooter talent adds, and only clearly non-zero at 5v5), and its coefficient wandered from ‚àí0.01 to +0.12 across refit windows. We left it out on principle: a game whose positional roles are increasingly blended is poorly served by a discrete role variable, and even today it would bias the defencemen who shoot like forwards, who are the most consequential defencemen. The position-by-distance shape stays on the list as a limitation of the features, to be addressed by describing the situation (possession state, traffic) rather than the player."]})]})})}let t3=[["xg-overview","Overview & four variants"],["xg-pipeline","Pipeline"],["xg-features","Features (93)"],["xg-base-model","Base model & diagnostics"],["xg-calibration","Calibration"],["xg-ablation","Feature ablation"],["xg-bayesian","Bayesian skill adjustment (EKF)"],["xg-environment","The league scoring environment"],["xg-talent-posteriors","Talent posteriors"],["xg-validation","External validation"],["xg-vs-conventional","Us, against the conventional approaches"],["xg-limitations","Known limitations"]];function t5(){return(0,a.jsxs)("article",{id:"xg-writeup",className:"prose-paper",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("header",{className:"mb-10",children:[(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"dateline",children:"Writeups \xb7 Shot metrics"}),(0,a.jsx)("h1",{className:"font-serif text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-2 mb-3",children:"Expected Goals, in full"}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"deck",children:["The xG model as implemented here: a penalised logistic base, 93 features, Extended Kalman Filter talent posteriors, calibration and cross-site validation. The full coefficient table, both eras, is its own ledger:"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"?tab=writeups&writeup=coefficients#coefficients-writeup",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"the coefficients"}),"."]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Byline,{})]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Toc,{items:t3}),(0,a.jsx)(e7,{}),(0,a.jsx)(te,{}),(0,a.jsx)(ts,{}),(0,a.jsx)(tg,{}),(0,a.jsx)(ty,{}),(0,a.jsx)(tv,{}),(0,a.jsx)(tj,{}),(0,a.jsx)(t_,{}),(0,a.jsx)(tS,{}),(0,a.jsx)(tE,{}),(0,a.jsx)(t2,{}),(0,a.jsx)(t1,{})]})}function t4(){return(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{id:"xg-star",title:"xG‚òÖ: what fitting the weights bought",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Fixed coefficients, whether Tango's original fifth-credit or the three-term wxG blend that followed it, are a modelling choice, not a law of nature. The obvious challenge is to let the data pick the weights. xG‚òÖ was that challenge: a non-negative least-squares blend over eight features (the four xG variants and the four disjoint shot tiers: blocked, missed, saved, goal), fit separately for each strength and side to predict out-of-sample G/60."}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"What it bought."})," Out of sample it beat a fixed-coefficient metric by about 0.010 of Pearson r and plain xG(sh+sv) by 0.015. That is directionally consistent but small enough that the interval spans zero, so we could not show the advantage was real."," ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"What it cost."})," 64 estimated parameters, a 4v4 cell fit on essentially no data, and a standing obligation to recalibrate and rebuild the shift table whenever the underlying data changed."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"It was taken out of service on 2026-09-06 for a further reason. Its weights were fit before the xG outcome-leakage fix, so they described features that no longer exist, and refitting was declined."}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"How it was fit",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The fit ran in two stages: an all-situation prior pooled across every strength and season, then a per-cell Bayesian update anchored on the legacy weighted-shots heuristic rather than on that prior, so a cell moved off the heuristic only where its own data insisted. The eight-feature space contains both the legacy heuristic and any single xG variant as special cases, so on training data NNLS ties or beats each by construction."})}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Why,{summary:"The result that outlived it: the four xG variants are linearly dependent",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The most interesting thing xG‚òÖ produced looks like it shouldn't be possible. The four xG variants are not merely correlated, they are linearly dependent: the identity xG(sh+sv) = xG(sh) + xG(sv) ‚àí xG(unadjusted) held at R\xb2 between 0.9998 and 0.99999 in every cell, with variance-inflation factors between 10,000 and 170,000. NNLS resolves that redundancy the only way it can: it keeps one representative of the equivalence class and pins the rest to zero, and which one it keeps is close to arbitrary. Resample a cell's rows and refit 200 times and the raw weights hop between columns. On power-play offence the goalie column came out nonzero in 51% of resamples and the unadjusted column in 95%, while the model those weights described barely moved."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"That made the goalie-adjustment column read 0.000 in almost every published cell, which looked like a finding that goalie quality didn't matter. It wasn't. Reparametrized into the only combinations the redundant block actually identifies (total level, weight on the shooter adjustment, weight on the goalie adjustment), the goalie adjustment was applied at somewhere between 0.17 and 0.74 in every cell we fit, never zero. The zero in the raw table was a statement about double-counting inside a rank-deficient basis. A cell that weights xG(sh+sv) has already applied the goalie adjustment, and adding more on top would double it. It was not a statement about goaltending."}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["We should be careful about how far that rescues the adjustment. Refitting freely in the well-conditioned basis (the adjustment columns sit at VIF 1.1 to 2.6 instead of six figures) does"," ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"not"})," produce a clean positive goalie term: the estimates scatter from +0.77 to ‚àí2.13 across the six cells and change sign in three of them. The increments are simply small relative to the noise (at five-on-five the goalie delta has a standard deviation of 0.065 against the level's 0.465), so a free fit mostly fits noise. The defensible claim is the aggregate one: applying the full adjustment everywhere is worth +0.008 of predictive r over using unadjusted xG, with a 95% interval of [‚àí0.005, +0.020]. Probably real, not provable, and comfortably better than letting each situation pick its own."]})]})]})}let t6=e=>{var t;return null!=(t=["first","second","third","fourth","fifth"][e-1])?t:"".concat(e,"th")},t8=(e,t)=>1+e.filter(s=>s>e[t]).length;function t9(){let e=eP.filter(e=>e[6]<e[4]),t=eP.filter(e=>e[6]>=e[4]),s=t.every(e=>{let t=[e[1],e[3],e[4],e[5],e[6]];return e[4]===Math.min(...t)}),n=t8(eA.raw,eE.full),o=t8(eA.neutral,eE.full),r=eA.raw[4],i=eA.neutral[4];return(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{id:"ws-status",title:"What happened to xG‚òÖ and wxG",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Status: both retired."})," Neither is computed for new games or exists as a column anywhere on the site (xG‚òÖ was taken out of the API on 2026-09-06). What stands in their place is the headline pairing, xG(sh) on both sides. SAx (success above expected) is defined on it:"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{className:"px-1",children:"xgf_shooter_adj ‚àí xga_shooter_adj"}),"."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["xG‚òÖ is retired on the evidence in"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"#xg-star",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"what fitting the weights bought"}),". wxG is retired on the same standard applied to itself: scored against the plain xG(sh) inside its own blend, it loses at 5v5 offence, the largest cell on the site (",eD(eF.offence[eE.wxg])," against"," ",eD(eF.offence[eE.flagship]),"), and it is less repeatable than plain xG(sh) in ",e.length," of the ",eP.length," cells (",e.map(e=>"".concat(e[0],", ").concat(e[6].toFixed(3)," against ").concat(e[4].toFixed(3))).join("; "),").",t.length>0&&" The exception".concat(t.length>1?"s are":" is"," ").concat(t.map(e=>e[0]).join(" and ")).concat(s?", where xG(sh) itself is the least repeatable shot metric on the page":"",".")]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-8 mb-2",children:"The one it appeared to win"}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["wxG's only apparent advantage was on defence, where it posted the best held-out r of the five candidates (",eO(r),"). It does not survive a target with the goalie taken out: ",eD(r)," becomes"," ",eD(i),", below xG(sh)'s ",eD(eA.neutral[eE.flagship]),". The number that made wxG look like the best defensive metric was measuring next month's goaltending, not this month's skaters. The"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"?tab=writeups&writeup=ws#finding",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"Weighted Shots page"})," ","draws the two scorings side by side."]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Why,{summary:"Why the goalie adjustment leaks into any defensive number",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The same mechanism quietly undermines any goal-weighted or fully-adjusted defensive number, not just wxG's. The goalie adjustment in the xG model is ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"sigmoid(base_logit ‚àí Œº_goalie)"}),". That is not a neutralization: it does not strip the goalie out of the number and hand back a goalie-independent estimate of shot danger. It ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"incorporates"})," the goalie's own talent posterior directly into the shot value. A goaltender who has been stopping more than expected lowers every shot's xG against them; one who has been stopping less raises it. And a goaltender's talent posterior is, by construction, a persistent quantity, still mostly the same number next month. So any defensive metric built on the fully-adjusted or goalie-adjusted xG variant, or on raw goals, is carrying next month's goalie performance forward inside this month's number. It looks predictive. It is predictive. It has nothing to do with the skaters it is supposedly measuring."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["xG(sh+sv) shows it plainly. Scored on raw goals it ranks ",t6(n)," of the five (",eD(eA.raw[eE.full]),"); scored goalie-neutral it ranks ",t6(o)," (",eD(eA.neutral[eE.full]),"), behind even plain Corsi (",eD(eA.neutral[eE.corsi]),"). Nothing changed except whether the target still contains the goalie. xG(sh) does the opposite:"," ",eD(eA.raw[eE.flagship])," on raw goals, ",eD(eA.neutral[eE.flagship])," goalie-neutral. It barely moves, because it never carried a goalie term against in the first place, and that makes it the best defensive number of the five once the goalie is out of the target. wxG's defensive edge is the same artifact, arriving through its goals term instead of through the xG model's adjustment."]})]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-8 mb-2",children:"The lesson"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Every layer of sophistication here has to justify itself against what is already on the site, on its own numbers. Fitted weights did not beat fixed coefficients: xG‚òÖ's blend does not clear its own noise. wxG's blend does not beat the plain xG inside it at 5v5 offence. And wxG's one apparent win, on defence, does not survive a goalie-neutral target. What is left standing is the simplest thing on the page that still knows where the shot came from: plain xG, shooter-adjusted on both sides because shooter talent persists on both ends of the ice."})]})}let t7=[["xg-star","xG‚òÖ: what fitting the weights bought"],["ws-status","What happened to xG‚òÖ and wxG"],["true-sv-pct","True Sv%"],["goalie-denominator","Shots on goal as the goalie denominator"]];function se(){return(0,a.jsxs)("article",{id:"archive-writeup",className:"prose-paper",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("header",{className:"mb-10",children:[(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"dateline",children:"Writeups \xb7 Archive"}),(0,a.jsx)("h1",{className:"font-serif text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-2 mb-3",children:"What we stopped publishing, and what never started"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"deck",children:"Four ideas, each tested against a number already on the site under a rule written first. Two shipped and were pulled; two never reached the site. Here is what each test found."}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Byline,{})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("div",{className:"border-l-2 border-accent pl-4 mb-10 text-sm text-ink-soft",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"mb-2",children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Nothing on this page is a live column."})," xG‚òÖ, wxG and True Sv% are not computed for new games and are not available anywhere on the site; the shots-on-goal denominator was never computed for anything but this study. What follows is documentation, not a metric you can look up."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The reasoning is kept because it is reusable. The most useful thing xG‚òÖ and wxG produced was a negative result: the four xG variants are close to linearly dependent, which is why a fitted blend of them mostly returns one variant and zeroes the rest. That fact still shapes how the current model is built."})]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Toc,{items:t7}),(0,a.jsx)(t4,{}),(0,a.jsx)(t9,{}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{id:"true-sv-pct",title:"True Sv%",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"True Sv%"})," restricted the stop-rate calculation to straight shots -- unblocked attempts that were not tip-ins or deflections -- and added a rebound-control credit: expected rebounds minus rebounds allowed, valued at that season's league xGA(sh) per rebound shot, added to goals saved (and taken away when he allowed more rebounds than expected). The idea was that a tip or a deflection is mostly the shooter's and the screen's doing, not the goalie's, and that a goalie who keeps pucks away from second chances is doing something a plain save rate does not capture."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"We tested it on the review build, before it reached the site, against dFsv%(sh) -- the existing stop-rate flagship -- under three conditions fixed before the numbers existed: the year-over-year repeatability gap (True Sv% minus the flagship) has to be positive with its 95% bootstrap interval entirely above zero on every goalie-season pair; it has to be positive among goalies who changed teams; and the split-half correlation can't be lower. All three, in both the all-situations and 5v5 buckets, or the idea is not better. It cleared none of the three in either bucket."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Split-half: True Sv% 0.133 against dFsv%(sh)'s 0.162. Year-over-year on every pair: 0.084 against 0.121. Among goalies who stayed with the same team: 0.145 against 0.155 -- close. Among goalies who changed teams, where a real skill should still carry over: ‚àí0.063 against 0.043 -- True Sv% went negative. Dropping tips and deflections loses more signal than the rebound credit adds."}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"What the rebound-control term looked like on its own",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"RebAx -- rebounds allowed minus expected -- repeated at year-over-year r=0.23 for goalies who stayed with their team and r=0.02 for goalies who moved. Rebound control reads as belonging to the team in front of the goalie, not to the goalie who changes addresses, which is the same team-leakage pattern the shots-on-goal study below finds in misses."})})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{id:"goalie-denominator",title:"Shots on goal as the goalie denominator",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Every goalie number on the site is built on unblocked shots (Fenwick), which charges a goalie for a shot that missed the net on the theory that goaltenders force some of those misses. The alternative tested here: score only shots that reached the net, and credit nothing for a miss. We pre-registered the test (",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"backend/docs/goalie-denominator-prereg.md"}),") before running it: year N's value of a candidate has to predict year N+1's goals saved above expected better among goalies who changed teams, with the gap's 95% interval entirely on one side of zero, and the loser there can't also lose among all pairs. Anything else is a tie, and a tie keeps Fenwick, the incumbent."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["It tied. 844 goalie-season pairs, 637 who stayed with their team and 207 who moved. On target F (goals saved above expected per unblocked shot), Fenwick's r was 0.121 (0.052 to 0.186) on all pairs and 0.043 (‚àí0.120 to 0.193) among movers; shots-on-goal's was 0.122 (0.054 to 0.187) on all pairs and 0.031 (‚àí0.121 to 0.177) among movers. Neither candidate cleared the other's interval where the rule said it had to, so Fenwick stays (",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"backend/research/goalie_denominator.json"}),', verdict "TIE (keeps Fenwick)").']}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"The direct check on the team-leakage suspicion behind the question",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"A shot-level model of whether a shot misses the net -- distance, angle, shot type, strength, rebound flag, score state, period, season, and nothing about the goalie or the team -- predicts misses about as well as goalies appear to force them. Misses forced (observed minus expected misses per unblocked shot) repeats year over year at r=0.40 for goalies who stay with a team and r=0.08 for goalies who move, and at the team level it is r=0.58 for teams whose starting goalie changed. Stayers repeating while movers don't, and the team-level number staying high regardless of who is in net, is the signature of a team effect, not a goalie one -- plausibly each rink's own scorer habit in recording a shot as a miss, which travels with the building, not the goalie. It does not change the verdict above: misses carry very little xG, so whichever denominator eats them barely moves the number either way."})})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"text-sm text-ink-soft mt-10 border-t border-rule pt-4",children:["The wider argument xG‚òÖ and wxG lost ‚Äî weighted shots against plain expected goals, and both against the simplest thing that works ‚Äî is laid out in"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"?tab=writeups&writeup=ws",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"Does it predict goals?"})," ","under Shot metrics. The goalie splits columns True Sv% was tested against, including Fsv%, dFsv%(sh) and RebAx, are documented in the glossary's"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"?tab=glossary#goalies",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"goalie ratings section"}),"."]})]})}let st=e=>{var t,s;return null!=(s=null==(t=d.$Y.find(t=>t.key===e))?void 0:t.label)?s:e},ss=function(e){let t=arguments.length>1&&void 0!==arguments[1]?arguments[1]:1;return null==e?"‚Äì":"".concat((100*e).toFixed(t),"%")},sa=e=>null==e?"‚Äì":e.toFixed(2),sn=e=>null==e?"‚Äì":e.toFixed(3);function so(){let[e,t]=(0,n.useState)(null),[s,o]=(0,n.useState)(!1);(0,n.useEffect)(()=>{fetch("/data/a3z/methods.json").then(e=>e.ok?e.json():Promise.reject()).then(t).catch(()=>o(!0))},[]);let r=e?(()=>{let t=e.metrics.map(e=>e.gain_glm_vs_baseline).sort((e,t)=>e-t);return{n:t.length,glm:e.metrics.filter(e=>"glm"===e.ships).length,gbm:e.metrics.filter(e=>"gbm"===e.ships).length,base:e.metrics.filter(e=>"baseline"===e.ships).length,medianGain:t[Math.floor(t.length/2)],small:t.filter(e=>e<.05).length,maxGain:t[t.length-1]}})():null,l=(()=>{let t=null==e?void 0:e.selection_opponent;return(null==t?void 0:t.ff_per_game)&&t.shots&&t.primary_shot_assists?{shots:t.shots.league_gap_pct_of_untracked,assists:t.primary_shot_assists.league_gap_pct_of_untracked,fenwick:t.ff_per_game.league_gap_pct_of_untracked}:null})();return(0,a.jsxs)("article",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"dateline mb-1",children:"Methods \xb7 tracking data"}),(0,a.jsx)("h1",{className:"font-serif text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-2",children:"Filling in the untracked two-thirds"}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"italic text-ink-soft mb-8 max-w-3xl",children:["Corey Sznajder tracks a third of the season by hand. Here is how the"," ",(0,a.jsx)(i(),{rel:"nofollow",href:"/a3z",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"All Three Zones"})," ","page estimates the rest from who played, against whom and what the play-by-play recorded, and how far to trust it."]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Byline,{}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"Whose data, and who may see it",id:"a3z-provenance",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Every count on the page comes from Corey‚Äôs All Three Zones project: zone entries and exits, retrievals, and the passes that lead to shots, recorded game by game from video. It is licensed to Post Hockey and shown only to active patrons of his Patreon; we do not sell access to it, and nothing here is served outside that gate."}),e&&(0,a.jsx)(h.DefinitionList,{items:[["Seasons",e.fitted_seasons.map(h.fmtSeason).join(", ")],["Tracked games",e.n_tracked_games.toLocaleString()],["Player-games",e.n_tracked_player_games.toLocaleString()],["5v5 TOI agreement","r = ".concat(sn(e.exposure_check.r),"; ours / his = ").concat(sn(e.exposure_check.ours_over_theirs))]]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Why our 5v5 time on ice, not his",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The last row matters: our exposure is 5v5 time on ice from the NHL shift charts, not Corey‚Äôs own TOI column, because the untracked games only have ours. The two agree closely but not perfectly; the gap is a difference in what counts as 5v5, and it is the same in tracked and untracked games, which is what the estimate needs."})})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"The estimate",id:"a3z-model",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["For a player ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"p"})," in a game ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"g"})," Corey did not track, the expected count of a metric is"]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Formula,{lines:["mu_pg = TOI_pg \xd7 r_pN \xd7 exp(c + Œ≤ \xb7 x_pg)","","r_pN  his rate per hour of 5v5 in SEASON N, shrunk toward the","      position prior:  (Y/œÜ + a) / (E/œÜ + b)","Y, E  his tracked count and 5v5 hours, that season at full weight","      and his other seasons at œÅ^(years apart); where a context","      term ships, each tracked hour counts as exp(c + Œ≤ \xb7 x) hours,","      so the rate is what is left once that night's context is out","œÜ     how much one game's total varies per unit of its mean: 1 for","      a count, about an eighth for the xG-created columns","x_pg  what the play-by-play saw that night: usage, on-ice attempts,","      zone starts, own events, rush-shaped sequences, score state,","      quality of competition and teammates, opponent strength","","Season estimate = tracked count + Œ£ mu_pg over untracked games"]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The player‚Äôs own tracked rate carries most of the information. The context term is a ridge Poisson regression fitted with player effects absorbed, so it only explains why one of his games differed from another; it cannot invent a rate for a player Corey never saw. Position groups (forwards, defence) get separate priors and coefficients."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"How much a season leans on its neighbours is measured per column, not assumed: a season judged entirely on its own games runs worse than the old flat pooling across a career, so most columns still borrow from nearby seasons, and only seven of the seventy-six measured best standing alone. The xG-created columns also get their own noise correction, because a sum of shot values swings less from game to game than a raw count the same size does ‚Äî treating them as ordinary counts had been overstating their noise roughly eightfold and crowding out the player‚Äôs own passing numbers."}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Why,{summary:"Why a season borrows from its neighbours, and why œÜ is not 1",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"One season at a time."})," A rate used to be pooled flat over every season a player was tracked in ‚Äî one career average wearing five different dates, which cannot show a player changing and quietly rebuilt his 2022-23 out of his 2025-26. It is now estimated for each season on its own, with his other seasons entering at œÅ per year of distance. œÅ is measured per column, on held-out tracked games, and the measurement settled an argument: a season standing entirely alone is ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"worse"})," than the old flat pooling for every column ‚Äî two dozen games of tracking is too thin ‚Äî but nearly every column has an optimum short of flat, around 0.6 to 0.8 for the counts and 0.9 to 0.95 for the thinner xG-created columns, which lean harder on their neighbours because a season gives them less to go on. Seven of the seventy-six measured flat and ship that way. Where a player‚Äôs year-to-year change varies with his age by more than its own standard error, an age term rides along; for most columns it does not, and nothing is applied."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"œÜ."})," Splitting what we see into talent and noise needs to know how noisy one game is. For a count ‚Äî entries, retrievals, shot assists ‚Äî the variance is the mean, and œÜ is 1. The xG-created columns are not counts: they add up expected-goal"," ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"values"})," over the shots a player set up, and a sum of values wobbles about an eighth as much as a count of the same size. Treating them as counts overstated their noise roughly eightfold, which drove the prior so hard that a typical player‚Äôs own passing barely entered his own estimate. œÜ is measured from the data, per column, and is near 1 for the counts by construction rather than by assumption."]})]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"How the interval is built",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"The interval."})," With posterior shape ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"A"})," = ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"Y"}),"/œÜ + a, the rate‚Äôs log-variance is about 1/",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"A"}),", so for the untracked sum ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"U"})," the variance is roughly œÜ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"U"})," + ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"U"}),"\xb2/",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"A"}),": the sampling noise in games nobody counted, plus uncertainty about the rate itself. The œÜ is the same one as above, so the xG-created bands are narrower than they were when those columns were priced as counts. The page shows the 80% band; the point estimate is under the cursor and is what sorting uses. A player with six tracked games gets a wide band. That is the honest answer, not a defect."]})}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Why,{summary:"The season in progress, and its one known miss",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Estimated to date."})," For the season in progress the estimate covers the games played so far, and a player‚Äôs rate borrows only from his earlier seasons, never from later ones. A per-stat level correction then scales the estimated part; Corey‚Äôs counts are never scaled. Before it was shown, the whole recipe was replayed on 2022-23 through 2025-26 as if each were in progress, against a test written down in advance."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"The miss."})," Rerun on the October 2026 refit of the model, the test failed on one condition: one of its seventy-six stat-and-position cells missed its hard limit. For defencemen‚Äôs carries with a chance, the estimate came in 12.6% under the true count; the limit was 12.1% (the larger of 5% and three standard errors). Everything else passed, as did the separate test of the expected box score. The estimates were switched off on Oct. 22, then back on: the miss is small and it is the only one. Read a defenceman‚Äôs in-season carries with a chance as running low, by about an eighth in testing. The limit was not moved to let it through; the failure stays on record."]})]})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"Does the play-by-play help?",id:"a3z-validation",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The competition is a baseline that ignores context entirely: shrunk rate \xd7 TOI. Thirty percent of tracked games were hidden, by game, so every player in a hidden game was hidden together. Each metric ships the context model only where it beat the baseline on those games and its level was right; otherwise it ships the baseline and says so here."}),s&&(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-ink-faint italic",children:"Validation table not yet exported."}),e&&r&&(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Of ",r.n," metric-and-position pairs, ",r.glm," ship the context model, ",r.gbm," ship a gradient-boosted challenger and ",r.base," ship the baseline. The median gain is"," ",ss(r.medianGain)," of deviance; ",r.small," of ",r.n," gain under 5%, and the largest is"," ",ss(r.maxGain,0),". The full table is in the appendix."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"A few percent of deviance is not a transformation. It fits an earlier finding on this data, that shot-level proxies built from the play-by-play recover only about a tenth of what the tracking knows about a shot. The player‚Äôs tracked rate does the work; the context term trims the games where he played more, or less, or against different people than usual."})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"How much do the tracked games tell you?",id:"a3z-reliability",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Split-half reliability of the raw per-60 rates within a player-season, tracked games divided at random, Spearman-Brown corrected to the full tracked sample. Low numbers mean the metric needs many games before it says something about the player rather than the games."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The lists are in the appendix."})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"Is the tracked sample representative?",id:"a3z-selection",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Corey chooses which games to track, so the tracked third is not a random draw. On the things the play-by-play can see, the difference between a player‚Äôs tracked and untracked games is:"}),e&&(0,a.jsx)(h.DefinitionList,{items:Object.entries(e.selection).filter(e=>{let[,t]=e;return"object"==typeof t}).map(e=>{let[t,s]=e;return[t,"".concat(s.mean_tracked_minus_untracked>=0?"+":"").concat(sa(s.mean_tracked_minus_untracked)," (").concat(ss(s.as_share_of_untracked_mean)," of the untracked mean)")]})}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The context features exist partly to absorb this: if his tracked games ran hotter than a player‚Äôs season, the model sees the lower on-ice attempts and TOI in the untracked games and scales down. It cannot absorb selection on things the play-by-play does not record ‚Äî which is why the table below exists."}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Whom he tracked them against."})," The check above covers quantities the play-by-play carries for every game, so the model can already see those skews. This one covers the opposition in Corey‚Äôs own terms, which it cannot. Each row is the difference between the opponents faced in his tracked games and in the untracked ones, as a share of the untracked mean and as a fraction of the spread between teams."]}),(null==e?void 0:e.selection_opponent)&&(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"overflow-x-auto my-4",children:(0,a.jsxs)("table",{className:"text-sm w-full",children:[(0,a.jsx)("thead",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"text-left text-ink-faint border-b border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"py-1 pr-3",children:"Opponent‚Äôs..."}),(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"pr-3 text-right",children:"Gap"}),(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"pr-3 text-right",children:"As a share of team spread"}),(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"pr-3 text-right",children:"Worst 5% of team-seasons"})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("tbody",{children:Object.entries(e.selection_opponent).sort((e,t)=>Math.abs(t[1].league_gap_in_team_sd)-Math.abs(e[1].league_gap_in_team_sd)).map(e=>{let[t,s]=e;return(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"py-1 pr-3",children:s.label}),(0,a.jsxs)("td",{className:"pr-3 text-right tabular-nums",children:[s.league_gap_pct_of_untracked>=0?"+":"",s.league_gap_pct_of_untracked.toFixed(2),"%"]}),(0,a.jsxs)("td",{className:"pr-3 text-right tabular-nums",children:[s.league_gap_in_team_sd>=0?"+":"",s.league_gap_in_team_sd.toFixed(3)]}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"pr-3 text-right tabular-nums",children:s.team_season_gap_p95_in_team_sd.toFixed(2)})]},t)})})]})}),l&&(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Read it against the control line, opponent Fenwick-for, which the play-by-play does see and the model already carries. Most dimensions are flat. Shots and shot assists are not: his tracked games face opponents who generate ",l.shots.toFixed(1),"% and ",l.assists.toFixed(1),"% more of them, against"," ",l.fenwick.toFixed(1),"% more Fenwick. We do not have an explanation for that gap and are not going to invent one."]})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"The expected box score",id:"a3z-xbox",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The columns above count what Corey tracked. This section predicts something else entirely: the player‚Äôs ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"real NHL box score"}),". It exists because the two got confused once, and the confusion was ours."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The obvious move is to add up the expected goals on the shots a player set up and call the result expected assists. We did, briefly. Measured against actual 5v5 assists it came in at ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"0.65"})," for primary and ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"0.45"})," for secondary, and the band around the implied point total missed the real figure far more often than it should. The cause is not a modelling error and no modelling fixes it: Corey records a primary passer on about 74% of the shots he tracks and a secondary on about 38%, while the NHL awards a primary assist on nearly every goal. A pass nobody wrote down cannot be credited to anyone."]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"How the gap is measured and divided out",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"What does work is measuring that gap and dividing by it. Two quantities are observable for any season he tracks ‚Äî the share of his shots carrying a passer in each slot, and the share of his shots we can pair to a play-by-play event. Their product explains most of the season-to-season variation, which matters because a correction fitted separately to each past season would say nothing about next season. The residual is carried in the interval rather than assumed away."})}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Formula,{lines:["expected total = c_G \xb7 (own xG) + c_1 \xb7 (xG created, 1st pass)/(fill1 \xb7 match)","                              + c_2 \xb7 (xG created, 2nd pass)/(fill2 \xb7 match)","","fitted per quantity, non-negative, identity link: points really are","goals plus assists, so a model that multiplies them is harder to check"]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"One fit per column",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Each column is fitted against its own target rather than summed from the others."})," Adding a calibrated xA1 to a calibrated xA2 does not give a correctly covered xA, because the two are correlated and each band is calibrated separately. Six quantities, six fits."]})}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Validation is leave-one-season-out"}),", because the job is to price a season the model was not fitted on. Holding each season out in turn, the league level lands between 0.92 and 1.08 and the 80% band contains the player‚Äôs real total between 77% and 83% of the time, against a nominal 80%, across all twelve quantities and five seasons (research/a3z_xpoints.json)."]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Why the interval is not made wider to be safe",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"An interval that is too wide is not the safe error."})," The first version covered far more than the nominal 80% because it charged twice for the same randomness, and a band that always contains the answer tells you nothing about anybody. The width is calibrated to the claim, on training seasons only."]})}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Below five tracked games the model runs about 14% high, because the estimate feeding it falls back to a positional average. Those cells are left blank. An empty cell is honest; a number is not."})]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Section,{title:"Limits",id:"a3z-limits",children:(0,a.jsxs)("ul",{className:"list-disc pl-6 space-y-1",children:[(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:"The expected box score predicts NHL totals and is calibrated to them. The xG created columns count danger created on the passes Corey recorded, and sit about a third below real assist totals by construction. Do not read one as the other."}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:"Regular season, 5v5, 2021-22 onward. Earlier seasons use different workbook layouts and are not standardised yet."}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:"Thirty-two counts, twelve percentages and three derived counts (shot, rush and forecheck contributions, each a shot total plus its shot assists). Corey tracks more still; these are the ones with enough events per game to estimate. Percentages are ratios of two estimated counts and are shown as points, not intervals."}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:"Pass types split his primary shot assists by where the pass came from, so they sum to his shot-assist total rather than adding to it. Entry defence now carries the outcome of a target as well as the denial: carried in, passed in, and whether it became a chance."}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:"A player with no tracked games gets the position prior and a wide band, and is hidden by the default minimum of five tracked games."}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{c