 one rule. Score the player, shrink the score toward his position group by how little his sample says, and ask how much of what is left sits above the group‚Äôs top-decile line."}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Formula,{lines:["component z_i  = (rate_i - mu_i) / tau_i            on the group prior N(mu_i, tau_i^2)","score          = sum(w_i z_i) / sum(w_i)            w_i = split-half reliability of component i","posterior      = mu + B (score - mu),  sd = sqrt(B) se,   B = tau^2 / (tau^2 + se^2)","cut            = mu + 1.2816 tau                    the 90th percentile of TALENT, not of raw scores","P              = 1 - Phi((cut - posterior) / sd)","earned         if post is in the top ".concat(P?Math.round(100*(null!=(l=P.top_share)?l:.1)):10,"% of the group AND P >= ").concat(P?P.keep.toFixed(2):"0.35"),"               once earned, kept while P >= ".concat(P?P.keep.toFixed(2):"0.35"),"thin window    a shrunk score whose error comes from his own game-to-game spread, over fewer than ".concat(null!=(d=null==P?void 0:P.thin_window_games)?d:20," games: P >= ").concat(P?P.earn.toFixed(2):"0.50"," to earn and to keep")]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"In plain terms, earning a badge means ranking in the top tenth of the position group at the named act, with at least a one-in-three chance the ranking reflects real ability rather than sample noise ‚Äî a weaker, more comparable claim than simply ‚Äúmore likely than not a top-decile talent,‚Äù which is what an earlier version of the badges implied."}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The exception is the thin window. Most of our standard errors come from the spread of a player‚Äôs own game-to-game results, and over a dozen games that spread understates how little is known: a hot fortnight can clear one in three, and few badges earned that way in October were still held in April. So where a shrunk score‚Äôs error comes mostly from that spread and rests on fewer than"," ",null!=(p=null==P?void 0:P.thin_window_games)?p:20," games ‚Äî a call-up, a rookie in October ‚Äî the line is even odds, to earn and to keep. The thin player still counts in his group and in its top tenth; he only needs more evidence to wear the badge. The badges whose error is a count or a model already price thin evidence honestly, so the clause leaves them alone: the All Three Zones counts, which pool three tracked seasons (Motor answers to it only as far as its error comes from penalties drawn), Speedster, Netfront and Quick Draw. Nor does it touch the empirically cut badges below, which are not shrunk at all."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Level check."})," If the machinery is calibrated, the probabilities across a position group sum to a tenth of it. ",R?(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:["They do: the mean P for the shrunk badges sits between ",R.p[0].toFixed(2)," and"," ",R.p[1].toFixed(2)," in every season, and every skill badge earns between ",R.skill[0].toFixed(1)," and"," ",R.skill[1].toFixed(1)," percent of its group (",R.other[0].toFixed(1)," to ",R.other[1].toFixed(1)," ","for role and outcome badges). The stage that writes the table refuses to write if any badge and group with fifty or more eligible players leaves the band of ",sx.min," to ",sx.max," percent."]}):"Loading‚Ä¶"]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Three badges sit outside that machinery. The forwards‚Äô Finisher reads the talent walker alone, whose own intervals are too wide to deconvolve without collapsing the cut to the average, and Iron Man and Minute Muncher are plain observed shares with nothing latent to deconvolve. All three are cut at the empirical 90th percentile among qualified players instead, so their mean P plays no part in the level check above. The defencemen‚Äôs Finisher adds his own shot value per 60 to the walker and goes through the machinery like any other composite: the walker‚Äôs half enters as a known value with no error of its own, so the shrinkage acts on the shot rate alone. Until October 2026 it was cut empirically as well, which let an unshrunk shot rate over a couple of dozen games carry a rookie defenceman to the badge."}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"How the spread and the errors are estimated",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The spread tau is estimated with precision weights, so a tail of low-minute players does not swamp it; for rare events such as penalties drawn the plain estimate collapsed and every score inflated. Standard errors come from the spread of per-game residuals, the way Shift Performance computes them, or from a Poisson or binomial count where the source gives only totals. Spearhead and Sentry need a third method: a ratio of two pooled sums has no residual to take a spread of, so the error is a delete-one-game jackknife ‚Äî recompute the whole season without each game in turn, and let the spread of those be the error. A player needs ",null!=(u=null==P?void 0:P.min_units)?u:20," events and ten games before he is scored at all. The three highest posterior scores among holders in each badge, position group and season wear gold, silver and bronze: a rank among holders, not a grade."]})}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"The badge scored differently, and the two plain shares",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The forwards‚Äô Finisher is scored from an input that is already a posterior: the talent walker‚Äôs intervals are wider than the spread of its own means, since it is tuned to forecast next season rather than to be tight, so deconvolving it collapses the cut onto the average. That is why the walker is not shrunk a second time, and why, inside the defencemen‚Äôs composite, it carries no error: only the shot rate beside it is a noisy estimate. The two plain shares are Iron Man‚Äôs share of a schedule and Minute Muncher‚Äôs share of his bench‚Äôs ice."})}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Why the band is not tight at ten percent",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The band is not tight at 10 because three things legitimately move it: ties at the cut, the probability floor trimming a badge whose evidence is thin, and hysteresis carrying a player who has since slipped out of the tenth. The floor was 4 percent while every live badge had cleared the reliability gate; it moved when role and outcome badges began shipping ungated, because the gate and the earned share are the same phenomenon. Across the three seasons every gated badge sits between ",R?R.skill[0].toFixed(1):"‚Äì"," and ",R?R.skill[1].toFixed(1):"‚Äì"," percent and every ungated one between ",R?R.other[0].toFixed(1):"‚Äì"," and ",R?R.other[1].toFixed(1):"‚Äì",", so a floor set for the first population was the wrong floor for the second. A test holds the band so it cannot drift back."]})}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Why the rank, and what it cost",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Until 2026-09-23 a badge went to every player at better than even odds of being a top-decile talent. That is the textbook decision under symmetric loss, and it made the badges incomparable to each other. The probabilities were calibrated everywhere ‚Äî mean P of about 0.10 for nearly every badge in every season ‚Äî but the share clearing even odds was that tenth multiplied by how decisive the evidence was. On 2025-26 the empirically cut badges landed at 10 to 12 percent while Motor sat at 4.1, Scorer 4.7, Wall 4.8 and Forcefield 4.9. Nothing about those players was different; the components behind them replicate at 0.6 rather than 0.99 when measured season by season, and a noisy component cannot put anyone past even odds. Ranking first and keeping the probability as a floor fixes it in one place for every badge rather than by tuning any of them: the empirically cut badges do not move at all, because their cut already was the 90th percentile, and the noisy ones rise to about a tenth."})})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"What had to replicate",id:"badges-components",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-ink-soft italic",children:"Every reliability and validation figure from here down was measured season by season, on badges scored one season at a time before the window, unless it is drawn live from the data or says otherwise."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"A component enters a SKILL badge only if it replicates: split its games at random into halves of a season, measure it in each, and the two halves must agree at 0.5 or better after the Spearman-Brown correction to full length. Role and outcome badges are not gated on it ‚Äî see the three kinds below ‚Äî though the number is measured and published for them just the same. The weights in every composite are these reliabilities, so they cannot be tuned to flatter anyone. There is exactly one exception, and it is marked as one: Enforcer sets fighting, hitting and size level at 1.00 each, rather than at the 0.72 and 0.97 they replicate at, because it describes a style rather than estimating a talent."}),v?(0,a.jsx)(sf,{rows:Object.entries(v.pooled_spearman_brown).map(e=>{var t;let[s,a]=e;return{label:null!=(t=sj[s])?t:s,F:a.F,D:a.D}})}):(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-ink-faint",children:"Loading‚Ä¶"}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"The table behind the chart",children:v?(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"overflow-x-auto",children:(0,a.jsxs)("table",{className:"text-sm border border-rule my-3",children:[(0,a.jsx)("thead",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule-strong text-ink-faint text-[11px] tracking-wider",children:[(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-left font-medium",children:"Component, own data"}),(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right font-medium",children:"F"}),(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right font-medium",children:"D"})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("tbody",{children:Object.entries(v.pooled_spearman_brown).map(e=>{var t;let[s,n]=e;return(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule last:border-b-0",children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-1.5",children:null!=(t=sj[s])?t:s}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-1.5 text-right tabular-nums",children:sk(n.F)}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-1.5 text-right tabular-nums",children:sk(n.D)})]},s)})})]})}):(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-ink-faint",children:"Loading‚Ä¶"})}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Three things in that table decided designs",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Three things in that table decided designs. Hits replicate almost perfectly and run against offence (r about ‚àí0.5 with the for-side of xG impact among forwards), so a hustle badge built on hits would decorate players who never have the puck. Rebounds and rush shots do not replicate inside a season. And net penalties replicate worse than either half, because the players who draw penalties also take them (r 0.65). Later additions, measured the same way: competition faced relative to a player‚Äôs own team that game (0.93 forwards, 0.92 defencemen, against 0.69 and 0.55 for raw quality of competition, which carries schedule noise), competition minus teammate quality (0.98), share of the team‚Äôs penalty-kill time (0.98), and NHL EDGE bursts over 20 miles an hour per 60, whose year-to-year r is 0.88 with no correction at all. Speedster now reads the hourly burst instead (since November 2026): the top speed a skater reaches about once per 60 minutes, fitted from those counts and his ten fastest speeds, which holds 0.89 year to year for forwards and 0.87 for defencemen, against 0.89 and 0.84 for the count. A skater needs about ten weighted games in seasons where his hourly burst could be fitted, two hours of ice or more, before Speedster scores him."})})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"Three kinds of claim",id:"badges-kinds",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Three kinds, and the difference is the whole point of the house style, so it is drawn on the seal itself. A ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"skill"})," is an ink disc: a repeatable ability. Every component behind one has to agree with itself at 0.5 or better across random halves of a season, because a thing a player cannot do again was never a thing he could do. A ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"role"})," is a slate plate: how he has been deployed lately, and how he played it. It is a fact about his recent games rather than a claim about the man, so it is not gated on replication ‚Äî a coach who uses a player differently after Christmas has not made November false. An"," ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"outcome"})," is a hollow ring: what happened with him on the ice, luck and goaltending and bounces included. It is not gated either, and it is not expected to repeat; that is what makes it an outcome. The board and the player page group by those three and can be filtered to one."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["A skill badge is not a compliment either. It says the ability is real and repeatable, not that it helps: Beanbag holders block shots at a rate that replicates at 0.77 across random halves of a season, and blocking runs ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"with"})," shots against. Repeatable and useful are different questions, and only the first one earns a disc."]}),(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"border-l-2 border-rule-strong pl-5 my-6",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-ink-soft",children:"None of these role badges predicts goals, and none is meant to ‚Äî nor does Beanbag, the skill badge next to them. The badge is not neutral about winning. It is a description of a player, and the description is not a compliment."})}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"The window, and stars."})," Every badge is scored on the player‚Äôs last"," ",null!=(f=null==(t=g[0])?void 0:t.window_games)?f:250," games, and a game‚Äôs weight halves every half-life back. A skill halves every ",b("skill")," games, the half-life that best predicted the rest of a season on three held-out seasons. Usage and Outcome decay three times as fast as Skill: a one-season reading, by design. Roles and outcomes halve every ",b("role")," games, because a role is how a player is used now and an outcome is what happened lately, not who he has been. Badges built from season totals (tracking data, EDGE, faceoffs, fights) weight each season by the same decay instead; that part has not been tested on held-out games. A seal carries two stars once the player has held the badge for a year without a break, and three after two years. Hover a seal for the date."]}),D.length>0&&(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Built and held."})," ",D.length," badges exist in the code and are not published, each for a reason it has to give:"]}),(0,a.jsx)("ul",{className:"list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 my-2",children:D.map(e=>(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("span",{className:"font-medium",children:e.name})," ‚Äî ",e.held_reason]},e.key))})]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Outcomes: Scorer and Ledger",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Outcomes"})," count what happened, not what he did. ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"Scorer"})," is goals per 60 at 5v5 and on the power play ‚Äî the two states a scoring role is actually played in. It is deliberately not Finisher: Finisher reads the shooting posterior behind GAx(sh) and says who ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"should"})," score, and every season the two lists disagree, because a goal is a shot plus a goalie plus a bounce. Defencemen do not get a Scorer seal; their goal rate agreed with itself at 0.47 across random halves of a season, under the bar, because a dozen goals cannot be told from luck. ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"Ledger"})," is the on-ice goal difference, measured against what his own team managed with him on the bench ‚Äî the honest version of plus-minus, and still not a skill: his goalie‚Äôs night and his shooters‚Äô night are both in it."]})}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Roles: how a player is used, or how he plays",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Roles"})," say how a player is used, or how he plays, and they make no claim about whether it works. The ones about ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"use"})," are all measured against his own bench, because deployment is one coach dividing one team‚Äôs ice and a share needs a bench to be a share of. ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"Enforcer"})," is the exception and the reason the sentence says ‚Äúor how he plays‚Äù: it is a style rather than a deployment, and fighting, hitting, sitting in the box and being big are things a man does and is on any roster, so they are scored against the league‚Äôs position group instead."]})}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Hard Minutes",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"Hard Minutes"})," asks who a coach sends out against the other side‚Äôs best, with less help, and on the kill. All three of its components are measured against his own team and position in that game, and the middle one only became so on 2026-09-23. Before that, competition minus teammates was a raw difference ‚Äî and on a bad team every player‚Äôs teammates are bad, so the whole roster scored well. It correlated ‚àí0.69 with team win share for forwards and ‚àí0.73 for defencemen, and dragged the badge to ‚àí0.54: nine earners each on Chicago and Vancouver, none on Colorado or Buffalo. Taking the team mean out leaves the question the badge always claimed to ask ‚Äî which players on THIS bench draw the hard assignment ‚Äî and the correlation with team quality falls to +0.09."]})}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Minute Muncher",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"Minute Muncher"})," is his share of the ice his own team gave his position group: the most repeatable number on the site at 0.99, and a statement about the coach, not the player. It was raw ice time per game until 2026-09-23, and making it a share changed nothing at all ‚Äî the two correlate 1.00 within a position group in all three seasons, because a team‚Äôs forwards split about 180 minutes a night and its defencemen about 120 whatever kind of team it is. The denominator is right now, and it was never the problem."]})}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Iron Man",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"Iron Man"})," is games played over the last three seasons as a share of his team‚Äôs, and is the only badge that looks past the current one; a player who has not been in the league all three years is not scored at all, because a rookie at 82 of 82 has not made the claim yet."]})}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Spearhead and Sentry",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"Spearhead"})," and ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"Sentry"})," read the implied purpose of a player‚Äôs shifts ‚Äî how often the bench sent him out to chase or to hold ‚Äî and, since 2026-09-23, they read it ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"against his own bench"}),". Each compares, inside every game, his share of his team‚Äôs attack- or defend-purpose 5v5 ice with his share of the rest of its 5v5 ice, and pools the games: one when the coach turns to him for that job exactly as often as he turns to him for everything else, above one when he reaches for him. A night his team never spent defending tells you nothing about who it trusts to defend, so it adds nothing to either side. The first version asked what share of a player‚Äôs own shifts carried the purpose, and that is a fact about his team before it is a fact about him. A team that leads a lot defends a lot; a team that trails a lot attacks a lot. Sentry‚Äôs per-team earner count correlated +0.37 to +0.55 with team win share and Spearhead‚Äôs ‚àí0.47 to ‚àí0.69, and in 2025-26 three fifths of every Spearhead in the league sat on one roster. Against the bench, on the 250-game window over 2023-24 to 2025-26, the largest share on any one team falls to between one in fifteen and one in seven, and the correlation with winning to between ‚àí0.13 and +0.22. The new number is a different one rather than a rescaled one ‚Äî the two agree at 0.08 to 0.26 ‚Äî and, against expectation, it mostly repeats better: 0.68 and 0.68 for Spearhead, 0.59 and 0.42 for Sentry, against 0.46/0.43 and 0.48/0.41 before. Taking a team out removed a large and perfectly stable term, and what was left still repeated more, which is what a real difference between the men on one bench looks like. Both were held on the old number until 2026-09-23, on a gate that was never the right test for a role badge."]})}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Why,{summary:"The estimator that decided Spearhead and Sentry",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"One more thing about the aggregation, because it decided the badge. A per-game ratio divides one small noisy number by another, and a season figure built by averaging those ratios chases whichever night went strangest: measured that way the component replicates at 0.17 and 0.31 for Spearhead and 0.36 and 0.22 for Sentry, and the badge then went to between seven and fifteen forwards in a league of five hundred ‚Äî one and a half to three percent, against the tenth it promises ‚Äî because nothing measured that badly can clear the confidence floor. The published figure pools first and divides once, summing over the games before it takes the ratio, so a two-second night counts for two seconds; it replicates one and a half to four times better. Same definition, same data, a different estimator, and it is the whole difference between a badge and noise."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The pooled version had a flaw of its own, found on 2026-10-09. It divided his share of the purpose ice by his share of all the ice in every game he played, including games his team never spent in the purpose at all. A forward who played five minutes of a night his team never spent defending saw his ordinary share fall and his Sentry rise, and that one game moved him past the confidence floor: playing little where there was nothing to defend made him look trusted to defend. The badge now compares the two shares inside each game ‚Äî his share of the purpose ice against his share of the rest of that night‚Äôs ice ‚Äî and pools the games (a Mantel‚ÄìHaenszel ratio, the game as the stratum). A night with no purpose ice, or nothing but, adds nothing to either side. What the old form also counted, and this one does not, is whether he got a bigger slice of the nights that held more of the purpose: a real pattern, mostly depth forwards who play more on the nights their team spends defending, but a fact about which games he played, not about how the bench used him inside one. It also carried some of the team back in: on the window, the old Sentry‚Äôs per-team earner count ran up to +0.53 with team win share, the new one to +0.22. Dropping it costs Sentry some repeatability ‚Äî 0.59 and 0.42 against 0.65 and 0.56 ‚Äî and Spearhead almost none, 0.68 and 0.68 against 0.72 and 0.68, and it makes the two badges less of a mirror of each other."})]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Enforcer",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"Enforcer"})," is a composite, not a fight count: fighting majors, hits per 60, penalty minutes per 60 and size, over every skater at his position. It had an eligibility floor of three fights until 2026-09-23, which made it a ranking of fighters and left a tenth of the twenty defencemen who fight at two men. The fight does its work through the weight now instead. Fighting, hitting and size are the three things the badge is about and they are weighted level, 27% of the score each, with penalty minutes taking the remaining 20% at their measured reliability. Those three equal weights are the only numbers in the badge system chosen rather than measured ‚Äî fighting replicates at 0.72 and hitting at 0.97, and they are set equal anyway, because the badge is a description of a style rather than an estimate of a talent. The fight count enters as its square root, so the first fight is worth most, about eight tenths of a standard deviation on its own, and a tenth one cannot run away with the badge. You do not have to fight to earn it: Sammy Blais threw 26.5 hits per 60 without dropping the gloves once and is a holder. But a fighter with ordinary hits still outranks a heavy hitter who never fought ‚Äî Logan Stanley fought eight times, hit 5.2 times per 60 and is fourth among defencemen ‚Äî and that is the balance the square root buys."]})}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Why,{summary:"Quick Draw",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"Quick Draw"})," rates a centre‚Äôs faceoffs, and says out loud that the rating is nearly the raw number. Every draw is a contest between two men, so it is fitted as a Bradley-Terry model over the draws themselves, which adjusts for who each centre lined up against; and each draw is weighted by what it is worth. That weight is the expected-goal swing riding on the outcome over the following fifteen seconds, which is where the even-strength effect has saturated:"]}),(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"overflow-x-auto",children:(0,a.jsxs)("table",{className:"text-sm border border-rule my-3",children:[(0,a.jsx)("thead",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule-strong text-ink-faint text-[11px] tracking-wider",children:[(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-left font-medium",children:"Draw"}),(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"px-3 py-2 text-right font-medium",children:"xG swing, 15s"})]})}),(0,a.jsxs)("tbody",{children:[(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-1.5",children:"End zone, even strength"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-1.5 text-right tabular-nums",children:"0.0157"})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-1.5",children:"Neutral zone, even strength"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-1.5 text-right tabular-nums",children:"0.0059"})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-1.5",children:"End zone, special teams"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-1.5 text-right tabular-nums",children:"0.0247"})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-1.5",children:"Neutral zone, special teams"}),(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"px-3 py-1.5 text-right tabular-nums",children:"0.0051"})]})]})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Two things about that table are worth saying. The stake has to be symmetric ‚Äî the same number whichever centre wins ‚Äî because our faceoff rows record the zone and the strength from the WINNER‚Äôs point of view, so the same physical draw is an offensive-zone draw for one man and a defensive-zone draw for the other. An earlier build weighted by the winner‚Äôs side alone and produced a rating that was offensive-zone wins measured against defensive-zone losses: it correlated 0.11 with faceoff percentage and had a winger eighth. And the spread is small. An end-zone draw is worth under three neutral-zone draws, and every centre takes a similar mix, so the weighting reorders almost nobody: the finished rating correlates 0.93 with raw faceoff percentage and repeats about as well across random halves of a season, 0.86 against 0.86. It is a better number, and it is barely a different one. The badge says so."})]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Beanbag",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"Beanbag"})," ‚Äî Target Dummy until 2026-09-23, and a role badge until the same day ‚Äî is the share of the shot attempts against him that he blocks himself, at 5v5 and on the kill. It was blocks per 60, which rewarded being hemmed in: a player pinned in his own end gets more chances to block one. Dividing by the siege leaves how often he is the one who steps in front, and it moves the list. The forward who stood second on the old count blocked 5.1 a night and was stepping in front of 6.9% of the attempts against him, the lowest rate of the three; he is off the new top three, and the forward now at the head of it blocks 10.7%. It is a skill rather than a role because that rate replicates at 0.77 across random halves of a season ‚Äî it is a thing he can do, and doing it is still not the same as helping."]})}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"What the role badges do not claim, in numbers",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["None of these role badges predicts goals, and none is meant to ‚Äî nor does Beanbag, the skill badge next to them. Blocking runs ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"with"})," shots against, because a player blocks shots in the end where the puck is: blocks per 60 sit at ‚àí0.32 to ‚àí0.40 with xG impact. Hitting runs against offence, about ‚àí0.5 with the for-side of xG impact among forwards, because a team hits when it does not have the puck. Fighting sits near ‚àí0.2. Minute Muncher needs a different disclaimer: ice time",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:" does"})," track play-driving, r 0.62 among forwards and 0.30 among defencemen, but that runs through the coach‚Äôs judgement rather than through anything the badge measures, so it is his opinion with a seal on it and not a second one. Those are the numbers for the share of the bench the badge now uses, and they are the numbers for the raw minutes it used before, to two decimal places. Every live role badge is pointed at the following season‚Äôs on-ice goal share in the table below ‚Äî holders against everyone else ‚Äî so the disclaimers keep having to earn themselves, and Enforcer‚Äôs does more than that: forwards who hold it go on to a 44 to 46 percent on-ice goal share the following season against 50 for everyone else, at p 0.001 and 0.011. Sentry does the same thing now that it is measured against the bench: forwards who hold it go on to 42 and 44 percent against 50, at p 0.0004 and 0.0007, and Spearhead‚Äôs forwards to 53 and 52. That is the badges finding the fourth line and the first line, which is what a deployment badge should find, and it is also the clearest statement available that they are not compliments. The badge is not neutral about winning. It is a description of a player, and the description is not a compliment. They are here because professionals look at them, and because describing a player is a different job from rating one. Penalty minutes appear as one component of Enforcer and nowhere else: there is no penalty-minute badge, and there is not going to be."]})})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"How the badges relate: the crosstab",id:"badges-crosstab",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Each badge is meant to name a different act, but the set is not independent. A few links are by design: Forcefield‚Äôs only component is one of Ice Tilter‚Äôs two, and Spearhead and Sentry read the same purpose label from its two ends. Beyond those, good players do several things, so badges travel together. The table is the correlation between badge scores",A?", ".concat((0,h.fmtSeason)(Number(A))):"","."]}),y&&A&&g.length>0?sw.map(e=>{let[t,s]=e;return y.seasons[A].redundancy[t]?(0,a.jsxs)("div",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-5 mb-1",children:s}),(0,a.jsx)(sT,{matrix:y.seasons[A].redundancy[t],defs:g,group:t})]},t):null}):(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-ink-faint",children:"Loading‚Ä¶"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"A study of all three public seasons, forwards and defencemen separately, found this."}),(0,a.jsxs)("ul",{className:"list-disc ml-6 space-y-2 my-3 text-ink-soft",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"No badge is a copy of another."})," We set the line for ‚Äúnear-redundant‚Äù at an R\xb2 of ",k?k.redundant_r2.toFixed(2):"‚Äì"," against all the other badges before looking, and none comes close. The most predictable is ",k?G(k.groups.F.top):"‚Äì"," for forwards and"," ",k?G(k.groups.D.top):"‚Äì"," for defencemen."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"One cluster is ‚Äúthe coach‚Äôs top offensive player.‚Äù"})," A single component carries ",k?"".concat(k.groups.F.pc1_pct,"%"):"‚Äì"," of the variation for forwards and"," ",k?"".concat(k.groups.D.pc1_pct,"%"):"‚Äì"," for defencemen. Among forwards it holds"," ",k?T(k.groups.F.pc1_with):"‚Äì"," on one side and ",k?T(k.groups.F.pc1_against):"‚Äì"," on the other (loadings of ",k?k.pc1_member_loading.toFixed(1):"‚Äì"," or more). This is why stars collect several badges. It is a feature of hockey, since coaches give the most ice and the attacking shifts to the players who score, not a flaw in any one badge."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"A few smaller families hold every season."})," Forwards:"," ",k?S(k.groups.F.families,!0):"‚Äì",". Defencemen:"," ",k?S(k.groups.D.families,!0):"‚Äì",". Everything else stands on its own in at least one season."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Some badges say something nothing else says."})," On the fit that includes centres, Quick Draw‚Äôs R\xb2 against the rest is ",k?k.quickdraw_centres.r2.toFixed(2):"‚Äì",k&&"quickdraw"===k.quickdraw_centres.least_predictable?", the least related to any other badge.":(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:[", low, though the least predictable there is ",k?N(k.quickdraw_centres.least_predictable):"‚Äì","."]}),"Among the rest, ",k?k.groups.F.under_035.map(N).join(", "):"‚Äì"," for forwards, and"," ",k?k.groups.D.under_035.map(N).join(", "):"‚Äì"," for defencemen, are barely predictable from the other badges (R\xb2 under 0.35)."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("li",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"The closest pairs are mostly pairs by design."})," Spearhead and Sentry mirror each other, and no player held both in three seasons. Outside the built-in links the closest pairs are Playmaker with Transporter and Finisher with Scorer, and even those share at most a third of their holders."]})]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"That is likely an undercount: each badge‚Äôs own noise pulls its correlations down, so true abilities may overlap somewhat more than the table shows. The effect is small for the two most predictable badges and larger for the noisiest ones."}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Reading the table, pair by pair",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Reading it."})," Ice Tilter correlates with most of the rest because it is the outcome they produce, which is why it is drawn as a hollow ring and kept out of the skill set. Playmaker and Transporter move together because the player who carries the puck in is usually the one who makes the next pass. We tried removing that by scoring Playmaker on what is left after carrying. It stripped the badge from Jack Hughes and Jack Eichel and gave it to forwards who pass in the zone and rarely carry, so the overlap stays and is printed here instead. Motor runs against Finisher among forwards: forecheckers and finishers are different people. Speedster is close to unrelated to everything among forwards, Ice Tilter included: fast is not the same as good. Among defencemen it is a different story. The fast ones are the ones who carry the puck in (0.58 with Transporter), and speed has a modest tie to results (0.29) ‚Äî both."]})}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Collectively exhaustive?"})," Parallel analysis keeps ",k?k.groups.F.k_parallel:"‚Äì"," components for forwards and"," ",k?k.groups.D.k_parallel:"‚Äì"," for defencemen, so the set spans several real dimensions, not two. The rest is linemates, deployment and noise."]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"What this study cannot say",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"It uses players with enough games to be scored on every badge, which leans to regulars, though correlations over everyone tell the same story. For the two most predictable badges the noise-driven undercount is small, so ‚Äúnothing is near-redundant‚Äù holds where it matters, but for the noisiest badges a low R\xb2 is partly noise. A player appears up to three times, so pooled figures are point estimates and stability is judged season by season. The three A3Z-based badges pool three tracked seasons, so adjacent seasons share tracking. Ice Tilter is still in progress and was kept in."})})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"Do they hold up",id:"badges-validation",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Three tests, the same ones the site used to choose xG impact over success. Split-half on the badge score. Award the badge on each half of a season independently and count how often the two agree. And ask whether holding a badge says anything about the next season: is it re-earned, and does the outcome it should predict differ between holders and the rest. ",y?"Twenty random splits per season.":""]}),C.length>0?(0,a.jsx)(sg,{rows:C}):(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-ink-faint",children:"Loading‚Ä¶"}),C.length>0&&null!=q&&(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Holders re-earn at ",L[0].toFixed(1)," to ",L[L.length-1].toFixed(1)," times the base rate (median ",q.toFixed(1),").",B&&B.clear.length>0&&(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:[" ","Against next season‚Äôs outcomes, ",B.clear.length," badge and position pairs beat the rest beyond chance (p below 0.05): ",B.clear.join(", "),"."]}),B&&B.weak.length>0&&(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:[" ","The weaker ones: ",B.weak.join("; "),"."]}),(null==B||null==(s=B.pm)?void 0:s.next_holders_mean)!=null&&null!=B.pm.next_others_mean&&(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:[" ","Playmaker forwards record ",B.pm.next_holders_mean.toFixed(2)," primary assists per 60 the following year against ",B.pm.next_others_mean.toFixed(2)," for everyone else",(null==(o=B.tr)?void 0:o.next_holders_mean)!=null&&null!=B.tr.next_others_mean?", and a Transporter forward's team generates ".concat(B.tr.next_holders_mean.toFixed(1)," expected goals per 60 with him on the ice against ").concat(B.tr.next_others_mean.toFixed(1)):"","."]})," ","Usage badges claim nothing about the future and are not tested for it."]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"The full table",children:y&&W?(0,a.jsxs)("div",{className:"overflow-x-auto",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("table",{className:"text-sm border border-rule my-3",children:[(0,a.jsx)("thead",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule-strong text-ink-faint text-[11px] tracking-wider",children:[(0,a.jsx)("th",{clas