, p ").concat(null!=(r=l.next_welch_p)?r:"‚Äì",", r ").concat(sk(l.next_r_score)):"role"===e.kind?"usage: nothing claimed":"‚Äì"})]},"".concat(e.key,"-").concat(i))})})})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"text-xs text-ink-faint",children:["Badges awarded on ",(0,h.fmtSeason)(Number(W)),", tested against ",(0,h.fmtSeason)(Number(W)+10001),". Base is the share of all qualified players who held the badge the next season. Split-half is shown only where a random split of games changes the input. ‚ÄúWalker‚Äù is a talent posterior carried across seasons; ‚Äúseason totals‚Äù are All Three Zones or EDGE counts, which a game split cannot touch (their component reliabilities are in the section above); ‚Äúcount‚Äù is a share of starts.",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:" Read the re-earned column with care for the tracking badges:"})," they pool three tracked seasons, so consecutive seasons share two thirds of their evidence and re-earning is partly built in. The next-season column is the clean test, since its outcomes come from our own play-by-play."]})]}):(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-ink-faint",children:"Loading‚Ä¶"})})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"What we tried and dropped",id:"badges-retired",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-ink-faint italic",children:"Tap any line for what happened."}),(0,a.jsxs)("ul",{className:"space-y-1",children:[(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("details",{className:"border-t border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("summary",{className:"cursor-pointer py-2 font-medium",children:"An 80% line, and then a 50% one."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"pb-3 text-ink-soft",children:"The plan asked for four-in-five confidence. The scores were calibrated, and at 80% a badge named one to five players in a hundred: a component that replicates at 0.6 cannot place anyone in the top tenth that firmly inside a season. The line went to 50%, more likely than not ‚Äî and that had the same defect one step smaller, so a probability line no longer decides anything on its own. The rank decides; 0.35 of probability is the floor under it, and even odds where a shrunk score‚Äôs error is his own game-to-game spread over fewer than twenty games."})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("details",{className:"border-t border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("summary",{className:"cursor-pointer py-2 font-medium",children:"A floor on shrinkage."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"pb-3 text-ink-soft",children:"Players once needed their own evidence to carry half the estimate. Brett Pesce led the league in entry-denial rate over three tracked seasons, stood at 96%, and was refused Wall at 0.49. The probability already prices thin evidence. The floor went."})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("details",{className:"border-t border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("summary",{className:"cursor-pointer py-2 font-medium",children:"Watch and carried states."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"pb-3 text-ink-soft",children:"‚ÄúWatch‚Äù marked anyone between 35% and 50%, which read as nearly there for players a third of the way. ‚ÄúCarried‚Äù held last season‚Äôs badge hollow until the new season spoke; once the floor went it applied to one player a year. Later, a badge belonged to a season and each October started from nothing; the window replaced that, so a badge now runs on until the evidence turns."})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("details",{className:"border-t border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("summary",{className:"cursor-pointer py-2 font-medium",children:"Spearhead and Sentry as a share of a player‚Äôs own shifts."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"pb-3 text-ink-soft",children:"That is a fact about his team wearing his name: a team that leads defends, a team that trails attacks, and the badges lined up with the standings ‚Äî Sentry‚Äôs per-team earner count at +0.37 to +0.55 against team win share, Spearhead‚Äôs at ‚àí0.47 to ‚àí0.69, and three fifths of the league‚Äôs Spearheads on one roster in 2025-26. Both now ask, game by game, what share of the team‚Äôs purpose ice he got against his share of the rest of its ice. No team holds more than one earner in seven."})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("details",{className:"border-t border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("summary",{className:"cursor-pointer py-2 font-medium",children:"Averaging per-game ratios."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"pb-3 text-ink-soft",children:"The first build of that fix aggregated the season as a TOI-weighted mean of the per-game ratios, which sounds like the same thing and is not: dividing one small noisy number by another gives a heavy tail, and the average follows it. The component replicated at 0.17 to 0.36 and the badge reached about two percent of forwards instead of ten. Pooling the four season totals and dividing once gets 0.50 to 0.64 and the tenth it promises. The estimator was the whole difference, on identical data."})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("details",{className:"border-t border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("summary",{className:"cursor-pointer py-2 font-medium",children:"Raw quality of competition for Hard Minutes."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"pb-3 text-ink-soft",children:"It missed Nico Hischier at the 86th percentile. Measured against his own team, with teammate quality and penalty-kill share, he is 94th and holds it in all three seasons, and the badge became stable enough to return for defencemen."})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("details",{className:"border-t border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("summary",{className:"cursor-pointer py-2 font-medium",children:"Finisher for defencemen on talent alone."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"pb-3 text-ink-soft",children:"It re-earned and predicted nothing (r 0.33 with next season‚Äôs goals per 60). Talent plus the shot value he produces himself predicts at 0.49, and its top tenth scores twice the rest a year later."})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("details",{className:"border-t border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("summary",{className:"cursor-pointer py-2 font-medium",children:"Forcefield against a coarse forecast."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"pb-3 text-ink-soft",children:"Without the competition and teammate terciles it went to sheltered third pairs. It now forecasts against the same fine cell Shift Performance uses."})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("details",{className:"border-t border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("summary",{className:"cursor-pointer py-2 font-medium",children:"Boomerang as retrievals per 60."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"pb-3 text-ink-soft",children:"It counted how often a defenceman went back for the puck and how often that led out of the zone, per 60, so it mostly measured how often he had to: allowing for the shrinkage, its score correlated 0.89 and 0.87 with his retrieval attempts per 60. Since Oct. 24, 2026 it is the share of his retrieval attempts, clean or botched, that led straight to an exit, each season measured against that season‚Äôs league rate for defencemen. The same correlation is 0.04 and 0.03 in the two seasons tested, the rate pooled over three tracked seasons replicates at 0.65, and a defenceman who changes teams carries it with him: the shift with his new team‚Äôs possession is within chance."})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("details",{className:"border-t border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("summary",{className:"cursor-pointer py-2 font-medium",children:"Imputed season estimates as badge inputs."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"pb-3 text-ink-soft",children:"They are already shrunk, so their spread understates talent; with them the entry-denial rate showed no signal at all. Badges read raw tracked counts, pooled over three seasons because a third of one season is about 120 attempts against a regular defenceman."})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("details",{className:"border-t border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("summary",{className:"cursor-pointer py-2 font-medium",children:"Netfront as production alone."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"pb-3 text-ink-soft",children:"The first version counted expected goals from within 15 feet and gave Timo Meier gold while he converted at the 20th percentile. Conversion cannot be its own component: at the net front it replicates at 0.17 in a season and 0.25 over three, and the fitted spread of true finishing there is about ten percent. So the badge is now one number, net-front goals per 60: expected goals on shots within 15 feet, tips, deflections and rebounds, times the player‚Äôs own goals-to-expected ratio shrunk toward the league‚Äôs by 87 expected goals‚Äô worth of prior. Meier fell from first to the edge of the top tenth; Hyman, Lee and Vilardi lead."})]})}),(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)("details",{className:"border-t border-rule",children:[(0,a.jsx)("summary",{className:"cursor-pointer py-2 font-medium",children:"Agitator, and penalties as a skating proxy."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"pb-3 text-ink-soft",children:"Net penalties replicate at 0.43. And bursts over 20 miles an hour correlate 0.13 with penalties drawn among forwards and 0.04 among defencemen, so Speedster (then built on that count) takes nothing from the box score. Bursts over 22 replicate well and are too rare to be a unit: a median forward has two a season."})]})})]})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"Sources and rights",id:"badges-sources",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Playmaker, Transporter, Wall, Boomerang and the second halves of Netfront and Motor rest on Corey Sznajder‚Äôs All Three Zones tracking, shown under an opaque licence: a badge and its probability are public, and no count, rate, score or posterior that could be turned back into his numbers leaves the server. His figures stay with his subscribers on the"," ",(0,a.jsx)(i(),{rel:"nofollow",href:"/a3z",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"All Three Zones"})," page. Speedster reads the league‚Äôs public EDGE tracking, one request per skater-season from 2021-22."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"On the ideas: shot assists as a better guide than points come from Ryan Stimson‚Äôs passing project at Hockey Graphs; possession exits and the retrieval that precedes them from Alex Novet‚Äôs transition work there; the defenceman compass, and the observation that denials are the most repeatable part of entry defence, from Sznajder himself. Knodell‚Äôs EDGE series at Puck Over The Glass found that burst rates repeat at team level and add little to shot share, which our player-level numbers bear out. Micah Blake McCurdy‚Äôs isolated-impact charts and Luke and Josh Younggren‚Äôs player cards at Evolving-Hockey set the standard for saying what a number is and is not; neither labels players, and the restraint is instructive. Outside the sport, the scarcity rule is Michelin‚Äôs, and the warning about badge inflation is every loyalty programme‚Äôs."})]})]})}let sG=[["Lightest third",.911],["Middle third",.976],["Heaviest third",1.023]],sF=[["Light by design (a backup all season)",2.2],["Cut short (demoted, traded, hurt or pulled)",9.9]],sA=[["All goalies",.9979,1.0092],["Ages 30 to 33",1.001,.9999],["Ages 34 and up",1.0284,1.0099],["Under 1,000 career shots",1.0003,1.0316]],sW={new:.12996,previous:.12972,diff:24e-5,se:84e-5},sP="#14130f",sM="#3a362e",sE="#8c8675",sO="#475569",sD="#9b2c1d";function sz(){let e=34*sG.length+40,t=e=>110+(e-.9)/.15000000000000002*470,s=t(1);return(0,a.jsxs)("figure",{className:"my-5",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("svg",{viewBox:"0 0 ".concat(620," ").concat(e),className:"w-full h-auto",role:"img","aria-label":"Predicted over actual goals against by workload: ".concat(sG.map(e=>{let[t,s]=e;return"".concat(t," ").concat(s.toFixed(3))}).join(", "),". One is level."),children:[sG.map((e,n)=>{let[o,r]=e,i=12+34*n,l=Math.min(s,t(r)),h=Math.abs(t(r)-s);return(0,a.jsxs)("g",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("text",{x:0,y:i+15,fontSize:"12",fill:sM,children:o}),(0,a.jsx)("rect",{x:l,y:i+3,width:h,height:17,fill:r<1?sD:sO,opacity:.9}),(0,a.jsx)("text",{x:r<1?l-6:l+h+6,y:i+16,fontSize:"12",textAnchor:r<1?"end":"start",fill:sP,children:r.toFixed(3)})]},o)}),(0,a.jsx)("line",{x1:s,x2:s,y1:4,y2:e-26,stroke:sP}),(0,a.jsx)("text",{x:s,y:e-10,fontSize:"11",textAnchor:"middle",fill:sE,children:"1.000 = a level forecast"})]}),(0,a.jsx)("figcaption",{className:"text-xs text-ink-faint",children:"Predicted goals against divided by actual, by workload tercile, before the age term. Below 1.000 the forecast ran generous."})]})}function sR(){let e=Math.max(...sF.map(e=>{let[,t]=e;return t}));return(0,a.jsxs)("figure",{className:"my-5",children:[(0,a.jsx)("ul",{className:"space-y-2","aria-label":"How generous the forecast ran on light seasons, by reason",children:sF.map(t=>{let[s,n]=t;return(0,a.jsxs)("li",{className:"grid grid-cols-[minmax(0,15rem)_1fr_3rem] items-center gap-3 text-sm",children:[(0,a.jsx)("span",{className:"text-ink-soft",children:s}),(0,a.jsx)("span",{className:"h-4 bg-rule/40 rounded-sm overflow-hidden","aria-hidden":"true",children:(0,a.jsx)("span",{className:"block h-full bg-accent rounded-sm",style:{width:"".concat(n/e*100,"%")}})}),(0,a.jsxs)("span",{className:"font-mono tabular-nums text-right",children:[n.toFixed(1),"%"]})]},s)})}),(0,a.jsx)("figcaption",{className:"text-xs text-ink-faint mt-1",children:"How much the forecast overshot actual goals against on light-workload seasons."})]})}function sI(){let e=34*sA.length+42,t=sA.flatMap(e=>{let[,t,s]=e;return[t,s]}),s=Math.floor((Math.min(...t,1)-.005)*100)/100,n=Math.ceil((Math.max(...t,1)+.005)*100)/100,o=e=>120+(e-s)/(n-s)*460;return(0,a.jsxs)("figure",{className:"my-5",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("svg",{viewBox:"0 0 ".concat(620," ").concat(e),className:"w-full h-auto",role:"img","aria-label":"Out-of-sample forecast level, previous estimator vs new: ".concat(sA.map(e=>{let[t,s,a]=e;return"".concat(t," ").concat(s.toFixed(4)," to ").concat(a.toFixed(4))}).join("; "),". One is level."),children:[(0,a.jsx)("line",{x1:o(1),x2:o(1),y1:4,y2:e-30,stroke:sP,strokeDasharray:"3 3"}),sA.map((e,t)=>{let[s,n,r]=e,i=18+34*t,l=n<r;return(0,a.jsxs)("g",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("text",{x:0,y:i+4,fontSize:"12",fill:sM,children:s}),(0,a.jsx)("line",{x1:o(n),x2:o(r),y1:i,y2:i,stroke:sE,strokeWidth:2}),(0,a.jsx)("circle",{cx:o(n),cy:i,r:5,fill:"#ffffff",stroke:sO,strokeWidth:2}),(0,a.jsx)("circle",{cx:o(r),cy:i,r:5,fill:sD}),(0,a.jsx)("text",{x:o(n)+(l?-9:9),y:i+4,fontSize:"11",textAnchor:l?"end":"start",fill:sM,children:n.toFixed(4)}),(0,a.jsx)("text",{x:o(r)+(l?9:-9),y:i+4,fontSize:"11",textAnchor:l?"start":"end",fill:sP,children:r.toFixed(4)})]},s)}),(0,a.jsx)("text",{x:o(1),y:e-14,fontSize:"11",textAnchor:"middle",fill:sE,children:"1.000 = level"}),(0,a.jsx)("circle",{cx:405,cy:e-16,r:4,fill:"#ffffff",stroke:sO,strokeWidth:2}),(0,a.jsx)("text",{x:414,y:e-12,fontSize:"11",fill:sM,children:"before 2026-10-01"}),(0,a.jsx)("circle",{cx:532,cy:e-16,r:4,fill:sD}),(0,a.jsx)("text",{x:541,y:e-12,fontSize:"11",fill:sM,children:"new"})]}),(0,a.jsx)("figcaption",{className:"text-xs text-ink-faint",children:"Predicted over actual goals against, out of sample, walk-forward, gated at 800 shots faced. The gate keeps only seasons that reached 800 shots, so it flatters the old anchor on low-volume goalies; read it with the ungated check."})]})}let sC=[["what-it-is","What the number is"],["age","The age term"],["censoring","The light-workload miss"],["provenance","Where it comes from, and what checks it"],["appendix","Appendix: the estimator and the checks"]];function sL(){return(0,a.jsxs)("article",{id:"goalie-forecast-writeup",className:"prose-paper",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("header",{className:"mb-10",children:[(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"dateline",children:"Writeups \xb7 Goaltending \xb7 Methods"}),(0,a.jsx)("h1",{className:"font-serif text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-2 mb-3",children:"The goalie season forecast"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"deck",children:"One number per goalie, made at season‚Äôs end and governing every shot of the next. It starts an unseen goalie below average, and runs generous on seasons cut short: a limit of the question, not a bug."}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Byline,{})]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Toc,{items:sC}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{id:"what-it-is",title:"What the number is",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["One row per goalie per ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"evidence season"})," N. The row is the forecast made at the end of N, and it governs every shot of N+1 (",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"forecast_for_season = season + 10001"}),", stored rather than re-derived). It is an information-weighted average of the goalie's whole history, each season discounted geometrically by how long ago it was, pulled toward an anchor by a prior. The anchor depends on how many shots he has faced in his career: a goalie nobody has seen starts below average, at ‚àí1.40 goals saved per 1,000 shots, and the anchor rises with the volume teams have chosen to give him. A goalie with thin evidence is mostly prior, which is the point and, as the light-workload section shows, also the whole problem."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Until 2026-10-01 the anchor was one constant"}),", set so that the goalies who went on to face 800 shots came out level. That tuned it on the survivors. Out of sample, the forecast ran 4.28 goals per 1,000 too generous on goalies with under 500 career shots, and 0.79 too generous across every goalie it covered. With the anchor fitted on every shot, whether or not the goalie kept the net, those read 1.04 and 0.01, and the first is well inside its error."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"A goalie who misses a season keeps his forecast."})," For the three seasons after his last one, the stage carries his history forward, discounted once more for every season he sits out, so the prior counts for more the longer he is away. Only a goalie with no NHL shots in the last four seasons is priced as an unknown."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"A positive number is the better goalie."})," It is published raw, with no display flip, and points the same way as the talent table it sits under."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"It is a rate, with no volume in it."})," The forecast is a talent per shot faced, and nothing in the table multiplies it by a projected workload. How many shots a goalie faces depends on whether he keeps the net, and that depends on how he plays, which is the thing being forecast. Baking in a projected volume would quietly use the answer. So the consumer supplies volume: multiply the rate by the shots you expect him to face, and state your assumption about his role out loud. ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"gsax_per_shot"})," is the same talent as goals saved above expected per Fenwick faced at league-average shot quality. Neither column is a season total."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"text-sm text-ink-soft",children:["The formula, the units and the checks are in the"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"#appendix",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"appendix"}),"."]})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{id:"age",title:"The age term",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Goalies decline, and the shape of the decline is now a smooth curve rather than a straight line with a break at 30. ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"It rises from ‚àí0.037 logits at 20 to a peak of +0.031 at 24"}),", holds roughly level from 28 to 31 (‚àí0.001 to ‚àí0.006), then declines to ‚àí0.040 at 35 and ‚àí0.094 at 40. It is a natural cubic spline with knots at 21, 24, 27, 31, and 35, linear outside that range, ages clipped to 20‚Äì40, and centred so a goalie of average age gets roughly zero. There is no break at 30 or anywhere else. In the table the term is applied to the output ‚Äî ageing is a fact about the next season, not about what the goalie did ‚Äî and stored separately (",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"age_adjustment"})," and ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"forecast_logit_no_age"}),") so any consumer can switch it off. A goalie with no birth date gets no age term."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Those are the curve's values at full strength, and since 2026-10-01 nobody gets it at full strength."})," A goalie gets the curve in proportion to how much of his forecast is his own record. The curve is an expected change for a goalie whose level has been measured, and its bump in the early twenties was learned from goalies who were already starters at that age; teams start the good ones young. Handing the same bump to a 24-year-old nobody has seen credits him with a selection he has not passed. Nico Daws, at 24.8 with a prior share of 80.7%, now gets +0.0055 of the curve's +0.0285."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Retired 2026-09-28."})," The previous version of this term was linear past 30 ‚Äî"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"AGE_BETA \xb7 max(0, age ‚àí 30)"})," with ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"AGE_BETA = ‚àí0.01054"})," ‚Äî chosen because the pre-registered primary estimator cleared its threshold at that size. The spline replaces it: it scores the same out of sample and does not assert a break the data never showed."]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-6 mb-2",children:"Chosen out of sample, and it is a tie on prediction"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:'The curve was fitted by weighted MAE against goalies who returned the following season and judged out of sample, walk-forward, with pre-season precision weights only ‚Äî the published forecast is for goalies known to be playing, so its target is conditional on survival. Draft pedigree as the shrinkage anchor, a team-change term, a "late ager" interaction, a sliding delta curve, a truncated-imputation aging curve, and a joint performance-and-survival model were all tested and rejected; none beat the shipped curve; most tie it within about one standard error and a few do worse. The anchor is fitted separately, by likelihood on every shot with no volume gate.'}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Walk-forward MAE among returners: ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:sW.new})," for the new estimator against"," ",sW.previous," for the retired one ‚Äî a difference of +",sW.diff," with a goalie-clustered standard error of ",sW.se,". That is a tie. The spline was not chosen for lower error; it was chosen because its shape is defensible where the old break at 30 was not, and it costs nothing on the honest metric. (That comparison is of the curve and the decay; the anchor's own check is the ungated one above.)"]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-6 mb-2",children:"What the 800-shot check sees, and what it cannot"}),(0,a.jsx)(sI,{}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Out of sample and gated at 800 shots, the 34-and-up bucket was over-docked by 2.8% while the curve applied in full, and reads 1.0% now that it is scaled."})," The same gated check reads the goalies with under 1,000 career shots 3.2% over-docked. That is not a new miss but the old one's mirror: the gate keeps only the low-volume goalies whose seasons went well enough to keep the net, which is the group the old anchor was tuned on. The ungated check, which counts every goalie, is the one that speaks for them. The decay is fixed at 0.85 per season of evidence, not fitted: a walk-forward profile from 0.7 to 1.0 was flat, and a freely fitted rate wandered between runs."]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Why the age term is in the forecast, not in the walker",children:(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The obvious place for an aging effect is the talent walker itself, at the season boundary, where it already applies a goalie aging curve. That placement question predates the spline and was settled against the retired linear term: fit through 2020-21 and score what follows, and the walker's 34+ bucket already read ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"0.9988"})," on goals against over expected, and adding drift moved it"," ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"away"})," from 1.000. It has not been re-run against the spline, but the question the check answers ‚Äî is there something broken at the walker's boundary right now ‚Äî has not changed with the curve's shape. So ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"talent_ekf.py"})," and ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"aging_curves.json"})," are still not touched, and this stage still triggers no refit and no cascade."]})}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Why,{summary:"Precedent, for comparison of approach",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Evolving-Hockey's public GSAx, as Luke and Josh Younggren describe it, carries no aging adjustment on the goalie side at all, and McCurdy's Magnus does not either. That is a precedent for leaving an age term out, not a test of leaving it in."})})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{id:"censoring",title:"The light-workload miss",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The forecast is level overall and about ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"nine per cent generous on the thinnest third of the workload"}),": predicted goals against divided by actual reads 0.911 in the light tercile, 0.976 in the middle and 1.023 at the heavy end. That gap is the most important honest thing on this page."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-ink-soft",children:"These ratios were measured against the estimator retired 2026-09-28 and have not been reproduced on the decay-spline estimator shipped since. The mechanism below ‚Äî a light season is light because it went badly, not because of anything the estimator did ‚Äî is about workload and selection, not about which estimator produced the number, so it is kept; the exact figures should be treated as approximate until reproduced."}),(0,a.jsx)(sz,{}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"It is censoring, not the estimator."})," Classify each light season by ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"why"})," it was light. A season that was light ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"by design"}),", a backup on one roster from the first tenth of the schedule to the last, misses by 2.2%. A season that was cut short, by a demotion, a trade, an injury or a hook, misses by 9.9%."]}),(0,a.jsx)(sR,{}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Those seasons are short ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"because"})," they went badly, and a forecast made before the season cannot condition on a season that never happened. The model is not so much wrong about those goalies as being asked about a set of seasons selected on their own failure."]}),(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"border-l-2 border-rule-strong pl-5 my-8",children:(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-ink-soft",children:"A goalie who was pulled, demoted, traded or hurt faced fewer shots because his season went badly, and a forecast made before the season cannot know that. Expect the published number to run about 9% light on the thinnest third of workloads."})}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Why,{summary:"The evidence that it is censoring",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["It is not the estimator. Simulate the goals as draws from the model's own probabilities and recut the terciles each draw, and the light bucket comes back at 1.001. The observed 0.911 sits more than nine standard errors from that. It is not a season that starts fine and goes wrong either: on the"," ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"very first appearance"})," of the season the light tercile already reads 0.934 against 1.052 for the heavy one. Two thirds of the gap is there before a puck is faced."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Read forward instead of backward it is starker still: a goalie who continues in the league misses by 3.3%, one who is demoted by 9.4%, and"," ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"a goalie whose light season was his last allowed 17% more goals than the forecast said"}),"."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"The only covariate that closes the gap is a leak."})," Offset the forecast by the volume the goalie ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"actually"})," faced in the season being predicted and the tercile span collapses from 0.112 to 0.024, so 79% of the gradient is repaired, while the stayer correlation rises 67%. Improving level and prediction at the same time is what a leak looks like in this frame; nothing honest did both. It is the one quantity that would fix the light tercile and it is the outcome itself, so it is kept as a yardstick and will never ship."]})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Why,{summary:"What was tried and rejected, and what survives censoring",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The honest candidates were run and rejected, and are recorded so nobody re-derives them. Prior workload and a short-season hazard model were both tested walk-forward; the hazard model is the better of the two and closes 0.017 of the 0.112 span for 14% of the stayer correlation. Inverse-probability weighting is not a candidate at all: reweighting changes which seasons the level check averages, not what the forecast says about any one of them."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"What survives censoring is small: among by-design backups alone, the three terciles read 0.978 / 0.975 / 1.025. That five-point gradient is ordinary shrinkage, since workload is assigned on talent and a Marcel regresses everyone toward the middle, and it is a fifth the size of the headline miss. A weaker prior recovers part of it at a cost to the calibration slope, which is not a trade worth making now."}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"text-sm",children:["The tercile figures come from the study run on the forecast ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"before"})," the age term, where the gated overall level is 1.005; the shipped table, with the term, reads 1.0097 on the same gate. The direction and the diagnosis are unchanged."]})]})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{id:"provenance",title:"Where it comes from, and what checks it",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The stage is ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"scraper/compute_goalie_forecast.py"})," and it writes one table,"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"goalie_season_forecast"}),". Nothing upstream reads it; the team projections, the lineup card and the goalie card do. It runs in the cascade immediately after ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"compute_net_rating"}),", so every stage in one run shares one set of season boundaries and one xG vintage, and it costs about thirty seconds."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"It is a re-derivation, and the re-derivation is checked."})," On the same 1,531 goalie-seasons, the production stage and the research estimator agree to a maximum absolute difference of 1.4 \xd7 10‚Åª‚Å¥ logits, median 1.4 \xd7 10‚Åª‚Åµ, which is 1.2% of one materiality unit. The stage raises and writes nothing when it produces fewer than 500 rows, when any forecast is non-finite, or when the gated or the ungated level ratio is missing or outside [0.95, 1.05]. The level check is read twice: gated at"," ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"800 shots faced"}),", the floor this project uses everywhere else, and ungated, by career volume and by age, because the gate selects on the outcome."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"text-sm text-ink-soft",children:["Full methods: ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"backend/docs/goalie-forecast.md"}),"; the age term in"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"research/goalie_context/step1_age.md"}),"; the censoring study in"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"research/goalie_context/lightload_level.md"}),"."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"text-sm text-ink-soft border-t border-rule pt-4",children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"One standing caveat."})," The forecast consumes the talent walker's own output, shooter-adjusted xG, as its evidence. Refit the xG model, or change the walker's priors, variances, aging or centring, and every number on this page moves, because the evidence underneath it moved. Nothing upstream depends on the stage, but plenty cascades into it: it is rerun after any cascade that touched"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"compute_xg"}),", and a forecast read against a stale xG is a stale forecast."]})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Appendix,{title:"The estimator and the checks",intro:"Reference material. Each drawer opens on its own.",children:[(0,a.jsxs)(h.Why,{summary:"The estimator and its units",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The estimator is the goalie's whole history on the production talent walker's own per-season evidence, each season weighted by its Fisher information and discounted geometrically by how long ago it was, all of it pulled toward an anchor set by career volume, plus a smooth age effect scaled by the data's share:"}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Formula,{lines:["mu(V)       = MU_ENTRANT + MU_VOL_SLOPE\xb7ln(1 + V/1000)","pred_no_age = (Œ£_k d^k\xb7I_(N-k)\xb7o_(N-k) + P0\xb7mu(V))","              --------------------------------------","                     Œ£_k d^k\xb7I_(N-k) + P0","","var  = 1 / (Œ£_k d^k\xb7I_(N-k) + P0)","s    = P0 / (Œ£_k d^k\xb7I_(N-k) + P0)","pred = pred_no_age + (1 ‚àí s)\xb7f(age)"]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"o"})," is a season's own one-step estimate of the goalie term and ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"I"})," its information, summed over every season of the goalie's history with ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"k"})," counted in seasons elapsed ‚Äî a season he missed discounts his older evidence just like a played one. The decay"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"d"})," is fixed at ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"0.85"}),", not fitted: a walk-forward profile was flat from 0.7 to 1.0, and a freely fitted rate wandered between runs. The prior precision is"," ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"P0 = 329.0099"}),", toward an anchor that depends on ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"V"}),", the goalie's career shots: ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"MU_ENTRANT = ‚àí0.022844"})," and ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"MU_VOL_SLOPE = 0.009883"}),", both fitted by shot log-likelihood on every shot with no volume gate. The anchor at zero is also the forecast for a goalie with no row at all. It replaced a single anchor of mu0 = 0.010673, which had been re-set to put the 800-shot gated level at 1.000. ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"f"})," is the age spline described above, and"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"s"})," is the prior's share. Each season's evidence is centred on that season's information-weighted league mean, because the walker re-centres the league at every season boundary; an uncentred cross-season comparison would read a frame move as talent."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"The sign."})," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"forecast_logit"})," is ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"g"})," in"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"logit p(goal) = pre ‚àí g"}),", where ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"pre"})," is the model's logit with the goalie term removed. Subtracting ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"g"})," lowers the goal probability, so a positive number is the better goalie. Scale: one per cent of xG(sv) is 0.01141 logits, and one tier is 0.08926."]})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Why,{summary:"The evidence gate and the stage's guards",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The evidence gate is ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"events.is_fenwick_for_xg"}),", a known goalie, and a final game from the model's first season on, regular season ",(0,a.jsx)("em",{children:"and"})," playoffs, which is what the walker itself consumes. A narrower regular-season mask exists and is used only for the prediction correlations, never for the evidence. The stage reads ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"events"}),", ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"games"})," and"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"players.birth_date"}),", and nothing else."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The research estimator reads cached frames that do not exist in production; everything the production stage does is rebuilt from the database. On identical inputs the two agree to float noise, pinned by a test at 1e-9. The residual is a vintage difference: the research cache's shooter-adjusted xG came from a different ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"compute_xg"})," run than the values in ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"events"})," today."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The stage ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"raises and writes nothing"})," when it produces zero rows or fewer than 500, when any forecast is non-finite, when the gated or the ungated level ratio is missing or outside [0.95, 1.05], or when the row count after the write does not match what was computed. A full corpus is about 2,245 rows, gap rows included. It also asserts that every input was knowable before the season being forecast: no row carries a lag or a career term it could not have earned, and the season key steps forward rather than back."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The ungated check is pooled across seasons, not a per-season target: a per-season ungated target drifts with the league's exit rate and would read as a model regression when nothing had regressed. Beside the gated check, the excluded seasons are reported rather than hidden."})]})]})]})}let sq=[["what-it-is","Two numbers, named apart"],["prior","The season-start prior"],["drift","How fast the season takes over"],["value","Does it beat the obvious?"],["level","Level check, and the known misses"],["band","The band, and the roster total"],["provenance","Where it comes from, and what checks it"]],sB=e=>"number"==typeof e?e.toFixed(1):"‚Äì",sH=e=>"number"==typeof e?e.toFixed(2):"‚Äì",sK=e=>"".concat(String(e).slice(0,4),"‚Äì").concat(String(e).slice(6,8));function sU(e){let{head:t,rows:s}=e;return(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"overflow-x-auto my-4",children:(0,a.jsxs)("table",{className:"text-sm w-full",children:[(0,a.jsx)("thead",{children:(0,a.jsx)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule-strong text-left text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-faint",children:t.map(e=>(0,a.jsx)("th",{className:"py-1.5 pr-3",children:e},e))})}),(0,a.jsx)("tbody",{children:s.map((e,t)=>(0,a.jsx)("tr",{className:"border-b border-rule",children:e.map((e,t)=>(0,a.jsx)("td",{className:"py-1 pr-3 ".concat(t>0?"tabular-nums":""),children:e},t))},t))})]})})}function sV(){var e,t,s,o;let[r,i]=(0,n.useState)(void 0);(0,n.useEffect)(()=>{fetch("/data/nr_forecast/methods.json").then(e=>e.ok?e.json():null).then(e=>i(e&&e.nr_games===Y.v?e:null)).catch(()=>i(null))},[]);let l=null==r?void 0:r.by_checkpoint.find(e=>0===e.cp),d=e=>{if(!r)return null;let t=r.marcel[e],s=r.per_game_var[e];return s/(s/(82*(1+t.w2+t.w3)+t.P0)+r.tau2[e])};return(0,a.jsxs)("article",{id:"net-rating-forecast-writeup",className:"prose-paper",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("header",{className:"mb-10",children:[(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"dateline",children:"Writeups \xb7 Player value \xb7 Methods"}),(0,a.jsx)("h1",{className:"font-serif text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-2 mb-3",children:"The Estimated Net Rating"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"deck",children:"One number per skater per season, made before the first game and moved by every game after it, printed with a band that has been checked."}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Byline,{})]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Toc,{items:sq}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{id:"what-it-is",title:"Two numbers, named apart",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("span",{className:"dropcap",children:"T"}),"he table ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"player_season_nr_forecast"})," holds two quantities per skater and season, and they are kept under different names because they answer different questions. ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Talent"})," (",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"talent_net"}),", with"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"talent_sd"}),") is the posterior mean of his per-game Net Rating, times 84 as published (the column itself is stored times 82): the rate we think he produces at, today. The ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"year-end projection"})," ","(",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"ye_net"}),", with ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"ye_lo80"})," / ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"ye_hi80"}),") is what his season Net Rating will read when the schedule is done: the games already played, as they happened, plus the talent for the games that remain. ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"The number on a card is the year-end projection, and the band under it is that projection's 80% predictive band"}),"; the talent's own sd is never drawn as a band. Before opening night the two are the same thing; after the last game the projection is the realised season exactly, which is the first check the stage runs on itself."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Both are built from the per-game offensive and defensive components that"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"?tab=writeups#net-rating-writeup",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"Net Rating"})," ","stores (",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"o_rating"}),", ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"d_rating"}),": goals above the positional average per game, all strengths), regular season only, and each component is forecast on its own. Goalies are not in this table; the lineup card takes their number from the"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"?writeup=goalie-forecast",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"goalie forecast"}),"."]})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{id:"prior",title:"The season-start prior",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Before a game is played we start from a player's last three seasons. The most recent counts fully and the two before it count for less (half to seven-tenths as much for the second season back, three-tenths to a half for the third; the table below has the exact weights). A season in which he played more games counts for more. Each past season is first moved to the player's current age, then the whole is nudged toward the average for players with a similar amount of history, as if he arrived with some extra games of average play (ten to 120, depending on the component). That nudge is large for a player with little history and small for a veteran. This is a version of the Marcel projection, Tom Tango's simple system (recent seasons weighted, regressed to the average, adjusted for age); the formula is below for readers who want it. Two things are unusual about ours."}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Formula,{lines:["m = ( Œ£_k w_k \xb7 n_k \xb7 a_k  +  P0 \xb7 Œº[bucket] ) / ( Œ£_k w_k \xb7 n_k  +  P0 )","a_k = mean_k + A(age_now) ‚àí A(age_k)         k = 1, 2, 3 seasons back"]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"text-sm text-ink-faint",children:["Here ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"n_k"})," is games played in season k, ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"w_k"})," its season weight,"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"mean_k"})," the per-game rating that season, ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"A"})," the aging curve, and"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"P0"})," the number of average games the nudge is worth."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"The reference mean depends on how much history the player brings."})," A skater with fifteen games behind him is usually a fringe player, not a regular, and pulling him toward the average regular was a measured bias in the first fit. So the average he is pulled toward (",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"Œº"}),") is that of players with a similar amount of history, grouped by games in the last three seasons: entrant, 1‚Äì40, 41‚Äì100, 101‚Äì200, 201 and up. An entrant has nothing but his bucket's mean and ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"P0"})," games of information. The entrant mean itself weights each past rookie season by ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"1 ‚àí exp(‚àín/œÑ)"}),", with œÑ of 30 games for forwards and 10 for defencemen: weighting rookies by their games let the ones who played full seasons, the good ones, dominate the mean, and a fresh entrant inherited a survivor-weighted number (defence entrants ran +1.37 high in the first fit). The œÑ pair was chosen walk-forward on entrant error with each position's bias held inside half a goal per 82 games (0.51 per 84); a parameter-free alternative, equal weights on each rookie's shrunk mean, left defence at +1.19 and was not taken. One caveat: œÑ was chosen per position on the same walk-forward folds it is evaluated on, one parameter per position, so the entrant cells are mildly in-sample; the league-level numbers are not affected."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["A player who has not yet played an NHL game has no forecast at all: the points projection counted him at the entrant mean and the team card showed a dash. A forward now gets a prior from his draft record instead: a regression on the log of his overall pick, whether he was drafted, his age and his pre-draft league (CHL, NCAA, Europe or other), fitted on every debutant's first season since 2011-12 with the same ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"1 ‚àí exp(‚àín/œÑ)"})," weights. Walk-forward over 2013-14 to 2025-26, it cut forwards' error from 4.58 to 4.26 goals per 84 and took the entrant mean's ‚àí3.86 bias on top-ten picks to ‚àí0.39. Draft position told us nothing about defencemen (calibration slope 0.14), so they keep the entrant mean. The prior describes a rookie who dresses and stays: only 24% to 61% of debutants, depending on position and where they were picked, reach twenty games."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Aging is applied to each past season before it is averaged."})," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"A"})," is a cumulative curve fitted on within-player year-over-year changes of each component (a polynomial in age, chosen by leave-one-season-out), so a 32-year-old's season at 29 is moved to what it would read at 32 before it counts. It is fitted here, on these components; it is not the shooter and goalie aging curve the xG walker uses. Survivorship is stated rather than corrected: a declining player drops below the pairs' 20-game floor and out of the fit, so the old end of the curve is flatter than the truth, and that shows up in the level check below."]}),r&&(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The fitted weights, forwards and defencemen, offence and defence:"}),(0,a.jsx)(sU,{head:["component","w2","w3","P0","Œº entrant","Œº 1‚Äì40","Œº 41‚Äì100","Œº 101‚Äì200","Œº 201+"],rows:["F_o","F_d","D_o","D_d"].map(e=>{let t=r.marcel[e],s=t.mu_by_hist_bucket;return[e.replace("_"," \xb7 "),t.w2,t.w3,t.P0,...Object.values(s).map(e=>(Y.v*e).toFixed(1))]})}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-ink-faint",children:"Œº is shown per 84 for readability; the model works per game. A defenceman entrant's bucket mean is the whole of what the prior knows about him."})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["On top of that starting estimate there is a small second stage, a penalised regression (the arm the holdout chose is"," ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:null!=(s=null==r?void 0:r.chosen_arm)?s:"‚Ä¶"}),"): age band and history bucket unpenalised, prior-season 5v5 minutes per game and power-play share, and the prior's square and its interaction with age band, penalised. It is fitted only on players with a prior season; entrants keep the starting estimate. It was kept because it beat the starting estimate alone for both position groups on held-out seasons without materially widening the worst decile bias (a twentieth of a goal of slack), which was the rule set before the fit."]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"Badges were tried and are not in the model."})," Prior-season badge posteriors as extra features could only be tested on the one held-out season where a prior season of badges exists, and on it they did not help",r&&r.badge_arm.length>0&&(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:[" ","(season-start MAE ",sH(null==(e=r.badge_arm.find(e=>0===e.cp&&"F"===e.pos))?void 0:e.mae_badges)," against"," ",sH(null==(t=r.badge_arm.find(e=>0===e.cp&&"F"===e.pos))?void 0:t.mae_shape)," without them, forwards)"]}),". The arm stays in the research script and reruns when a second season is available."]})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{id:"drift",title:"How fast the season takes over",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The starting estimate treats past games as evidence about his current talent. They are not quite that: talent moves between seasons. The uncertainty around the estimate is therefore the game-to-game noise in the games behind it plus a fitted season-to-season drift, ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"œÑ\xb2"}),", estimated by maximum likelihood on season-start residuals. That drift is what sets the in-season weighting:"]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Formula,{lines:["v0     = s\xb2 / I0 + œÑ\xb2            I_eff = s\xb2 / v0        (the prior's worth, in games)","post   = ( I_eff \xb7 m + n \xb7 run ) / ( I_eff + n )       var = s\xb2 / ( I_eff + n )","ye     = ( n \xb7 run + (N ‚àí n) \xb7 post ) / N              N = expected games this season"]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"s\xb2"})," is the measured game-to-game variance of the component for the position group, ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"n"})," the games played, ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"run"})," the running mean over them. The offensive component drifts; the defensive one barely does, so a three-season history is worth a few dozen games of current offence and a couple of hundred of current defence.",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:"N"})," is the games played plus the team's remaining games at the player's availability so far, and before opening night it is the length of the schedule (84 games from 2026‚Äì27)."]}),r&&(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:[(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"That asymmetry is a claim about hockey, and it is a fitted one."})," The drift variances, per game squared, from maximum likelihood on the season-start residuals of the walk-forward fit:"," ",["F_o","F_d","D_o","D_d"].map(e=>"".concat(e.replace("_"," \xb7 ")," ").concat(r.tau2[e].toExponential(1))).join(", "),". Offensive rating moves between seasons sixty to seventy times more than defensive rating does, so three full seasons of history are worth"," ",["F_o","F_d","D_o","D_d"].map(e=>{var t;return"".concat(sB(null!=(t=d(e))?t:void 0)," games (").concat(e.replace("_"," \xb7 "),")")}).join(", ")," ","of current-season evidence: a defenceman's defensive number is close to a fixed trait on this measure, an offensive number is a moving one. The defensive drifts sit at or next to the floor of the grid searched, so ‚Äúnear zero‚Äù i