sName:"drop-cap",children:"Every blocked shot in the NHL's play-by-play carries one location: where the puck hit a body. Put an expected-goals model on that spot and a slapshot from the point, stopped fifteen feet out by a sliding defenceman, is scored as a shot from fifteen feet. It was not. It was a shot from the point that never became one."}),n&&o&&(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The size of the error can be measured. In ",c,", valued at the block point, the average blocked shot was worth ",s$(n.mean_xg_at_block_point)," of a goal, against"," ",s$(n.mean_p_unblocked)," for the average shot that got through:"," ",(0,a.jsxs)("strong",{children:[n.ratio_block_point.toFixed(2)," times"]})," as dangerous. For a defenceman's shot at five-on-five it is ",o.ratio_block_point.toFixed(2)," times. Blocked shots are not more dangerous than the ones that reach the net. The location is the wrong end of the lane."]})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"A save by a skater",id:"blocks-save",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["So we treat a block as what it is: a save, made by a skater. The shot is valued where it was released, at ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"xG(sh)"}),", the shooter-adjusted expected goals. We know who took a blocked shot, so his finishing talent belongs in the number, exactly as it does for a shot the goalie stops. The blocker is credited with that value. The shooter is not docked for being blocked, and earns nothing for finding a lane."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"We rarely see the release point, so the value is an average: xG(sh) at every place the shot could have come from, each weighted by how likely it is. Not xG(sh) at the single likeliest spot. Danger does not fall off in a straight line, and the danger of the average spot is not the average danger."}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Formula,{lines:["value of a block = Œ£ over cells L of  P(released from L | block point, context) \xd7 xG(sh) at L"]}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["One exception. ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"A shot blocked by the shooter's own teammate is a miss."})," ","No defender did anything, so no one is credited with a save; it counts as a shot that missed the net."]})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"Where the shot came from",id:"blocks-origin",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Tap any dot. The cross is where the play-by-play put the block, and the oval around it is where the block really was: tracking puts ",s$(u.oval.share_inside,0)," of blocks inside it. The amber wedge is the lane, every release from which a straight shot through the block reaches the net between the posts, give or take a foot; it holds"," ",s$(u.core_share_pbp,0)," of true releases. The grey wedge is the aim lane, widened until it holds ",s$(u.aim_share_pbp,0)," of them, because shooters miss the net and the block point is not exact. The violet is the model's estimate of where the shot came from, drawn over the whole rink: the model is not confined to either lane. On the replay-checked blocks, the red dot is where the puck really left the stick."]}),(0,a.jsx)(al,{data:t}),t.context.frozen?(0,a.jsx)(aw,{d:t}):(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The heat does not stop at the blue line. A shot blocked at the top of the circles may have been taken from the point or from the neutral zone, and the model is allowed to say so."}),t.context.stick&&(0,a.jsx)(aj,{d:t})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"What the replays show",id:"blocks-replays",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["The NHL's EDGE tracking keeps the puck and every player at ten frames a second for the seconds before each goal. When that sequence contains a blocked shot, the replay shows both ends of the lane: the frame the puck left the stick, and the frame it hit the blocker. We read ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:l.toLocaleString()})," usable pairs from"," ",d.map(s0).join(" and ")," to build the model, and held back"," ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:i.held_out.n})," from ",c,", in ",i.held_out.games," games, to test it. The held-back blocks are the replay-checked dots above. No model on this page was fitted on them.",(null==g?void 0:g.n)!==void 0&&void 0!==g.n_censored&&(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:[" ",f.id," also learned from ",x," blocks where tracking found the release and the puck's flight broke beside the blocker, and ",g.n_censored," where it found the block with the puck still at the shooter's stick a frame earlier: ",g.n," in all."]})]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"‚ÄúUsable‚Äù takes in two kinds. Clean pairs have at least two frames between release and block. Short pairs have less, which is mostly a block at the stick, and they are kept only when the tracked block lands within five feet of where the play-by-play put it. Leaving the short ones out would drop most blocks made at the stick, and select the sample on the very distance being estimated."}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"font-serif text-xl mt-6 mb-2",children:"The sample is not every block"}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Replays exist only for sequences that ended in a goal. Those are not typical blocked shots. The replay blocks sit nearer the net, a median ",r.replay.median_block_ft," ft out against ",r.population.median_block_ft," ft for every block of ",c,"; they come more often on the power play, ",s$(r.replay.strength_share.pp,0)," against"," ",s$(r.population.strength_share.pp,0),"; and the long ones are scarce:"," ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:s$(r.replay.block_band_share[p],0)})," of replay blocks were in the ",p," ft band, against ",s$(r.population.block_band_share[p],0)," of all blocks."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The models miss most on shots released far from the net, and the far end of the ice is exactly where the replay evidence is thinnest. The random-sample dots on the rink are there so a reader can see what the model does with them. No replay can check it."})]}),(0,a.jsx)(h.Section,{title:"How good is it",id:"blocks-results",children:(0,a.jsx)(ab,{d:t})}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Section,{title:"Credits and limits",id:"blocks-credits",children:[(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Recovering a blocked shot's origin and valuing the block there follows The Bucketless (2021), whose method (where unblocked shots are taken, cut to the lane through the block point) is the old model on this page. The soft lane weighting that came before it is McCurdy's, at HockeyViz. The model in use is fitted directly on the replay pairs. The release and block points come from the NHL's EDGE puck and player tracking."}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"text-sm text-ink-soft",children:["This stat came from outside research. Have yours?"," ",(0,a.jsx)("a",{href:"/contribute",className:"text-accent hover:underline",children:"Send it to /contribute"}),"."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Blocks behind the goal line and shots at an empty net are outside the model. The replay evidence comes from goal sequences only, so its blocks run near the net, as above. The origin is a distribution, not a point, and the value of a block carries that uncertainty with it."})]}),(0,a.jsxs)(h.Appendix,{title:"The replay test, line by line",intro:"Each model was scored on the held-back replays against rules fixed before it was scored.",children:[t.models.map(e=>{var t;return(0,a.jsxs)(h.Why,{summary:"".concat(e.id,": ").concat(e.name,". ").concat(e.gate.passed?"Passed":"Failed",", ").concat(e.gate.groups?"".concat(e.gate.groups.main.n_failed," of ").concat(e.gate.groups.main.n_gated," main bars and ").concat(e.gate.groups.vs_a10.n_failed," of ").concat(e.gate.groups.vs_a10.n_gated," not-worse-than-A10 bars"):"".concat(e.gate.n_failed," of ").concat(e.gate.n_gated," gated lines")),children:[(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"text-sm",children:[e.gate.name,". Fitted on ",(null!=(t=e.fit_seasons)?t:[]).map(s0).join(" and "),".",e.predictive&&(0,a.jsxs)(a.Fragment,{children:[" Predictive: ",e.predictive,"."]}),"Source: ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:e.gate.source}),"; model file ",(0,a.jsx)("code",{children:e.artifact}),"."]}),(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"overflow-x-auto",children:(0,a